#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# scripts/mutate — run a mutation battery, and refuse to run one against an uncommitted tree.
#
# Why this exists, stated plainly because five sessions have already paid for it. A mutation
# battery applies a mutant, runs the suite, and puts the file back. Every hand-rolled battery in
# this repository put the file back with `git checkout -- <file>`, which restores from HEAD — so
# run against a tree that still holds uncommitted work, that step deletes the work instead of the
# mutation.
#
# It has happened five times: SONNY-40, SONNY-64 and SONNY-83, each written up in this
# repository's changelog; SONNY-161, in its own closing comment; and PR #67's cycle-3 fix round.
# Three of those did something worse than cost a redo — they produced false measurements — and
# they did not all mislead in the same direction, which is the part worth getting right:
#
#   * SONNY-64's three bogus "kills" are the dangerous kind. A false kill says the suite covers
#     something it does not, so the battery hands back a reassurance nobody earned and the reader
#     stops looking. Once, so far.
#   * SONNY-161's five and SONNY-83's one were false *survivors*, against code that had just been
#     deleted. A survivor is an alarm — this script exits 2 for one — so those made coverage look
#     worse than it was and got chased. Costly, but they announce themselves.
#
# Both kinds were caught by luck rather than by design, and a battery whose whole value is that its
# result can be trusted is worth less than nothing when it lies comfortably. (PR #70 review, F9:
# this comment previously said "twice ... in the reassuring direction" and counted a false survivor
# as one of the two.)
#
# Every occurrence was answered with documentation, and the last happened in a session that had
# already written up an earlier one. So the answer here is mechanical rather than written: this
# script refuses to do anything at all while `git status --porcelain` prints a line, and it has
# no override flag. `scripts/mutate selftest` is the proof that the refusal fires — it stands up a
# throwaway repository, dirties it, runs this harness against it, and asserts both that the run
# was refused and that the uncommitted bytes are still there afterwards.
#
# The second route to a false measurement, closed by SONNY-176, found by PR #73's fix round on
# 2026-08-19: a session started a build while a battery was mid-mutant, and the two shared .build/.
# A battery's whole output is which mutants were KILLED and which SURVIVED, read off compile and
# test results, so anything else compiling into the same build directory changes what is being
# read. A shared llbuild database or module cache surfaces as a transient compile failure, which
# this harness records as `KILLED (build failure)` — a false kill, the reassuring direction again,
# and one nothing in the report can show: a contaminated run prints the same counts a clean one
# does.
#
# Two mechanisms, because the two halves of that are enforceable in different ways:
#
#   * A run lock, kept in .git/ so it can never dirty the tree, held for the battery's whole
#     duration. A second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout is refused, naming the run that holds
#     it. It is taken BEFORE the dirty-tree guard on purpose: a battery that is mid-mutant has a
#     dirty tree by construction, so a second run reaching that guard first would be refused for
#     the wrong reason and told to "commit or stash the paths above" — and the path above would be
#     a mutant.
#   * A build directory private to the battery (--scratch-path, with the clang module cache inside
#     it). Nothing a shell script does can stop another terminal from running `swift build`, so
#     this stops that build from mattering instead: it writes .build/, the battery writes its own
#     scratch directory, and neither can corrupt the other's artifacts.
#
# What is left over after those two is written out in `--help`, under "What this does and does not
# prevent", rather than implied away.
#
# Usage
#   scripts/mutate <plan>                run every mutant in the plan
#   scripts/mutate <plan> --only M3,M7   run only these mutants
#   scripts/mutate <plan> --check        parse the plan and match every mutant; run no tests
#   scripts/mutate selftest              exercise this harness against a throwaway repository
#   scripts/mutate unlock                clear a run lock left behind by a killed battery
#   scripts/mutate --help                the plan format, with an example
#
# The third route, closed by SONNY-224 after PR #109: a kill this harness had no business
# reporting. R9 of a nine-mutant battery was recorded KILLED by exactly one test — a cancellation
# test that raced the wall clock — and re-run alone against a clean baseline, R9 SURVIVED. The
# mutant was never killed. A flake fired during its run and this script read the red suite as the
# mutant being caught, which is the reassuring direction again, and the quietest of the three: a
# battery that aborts wastes a run and says so, while a battery that invents a kill prints a report
# indistinguishable from a real one. `mutate-untrusted-failures` beside this script is the answer —
# the failures that carry no information about a mutant are declared there, matched against the
# issue a test recorded rather than against its name, and excluded from every kill count. A mutant
# with nothing else against it comes back UNATTRIBUTED rather than KILLED.
#
# The fourth, closed by SONNY-305 after PR #133's review: a kill whose evidence the report threw
# away. swift-testing names a failing test on two different line shapes — the per-test summary
# ("Test <name> failed after ...") and each issue it records ("Test <name> recorded an issue at
# ...") — and this script read only the first. When a run records the issues and never prints the
# summary line, the mutant was reported "KILLED — the run failed but named no test", advising a
# reader to look for a crash that never happened while the log named the killing test five times a
# few lines up. In the case that was reported the count was right and only the evidence was gone,
# which is bad enough — the whole reason to run a battery is to learn WHICH test holds a behaviour.
# But the same silence had a second direction that IS a wrong count, and it is why this is numbered
# beside the other three rather than filed as a misdiagnosis: when the only red was a DECLARED
# failure, the classifier saw nothing at all and so could not report UNATTRIBUTED, and the mutant
# was counted KILLED. That is the manufactured kill the third route above built its whole
# declaration mechanism to prevent, arriving by a road that mechanism never watched. Not observed
# in the wild — the reported case is the lost-evidence one — but reachable, and reproduced against
# the previous version of this script rather than argued; `selftest` has an arm on each direction.
# It is also intermittent — the same mutant at the same tree attributed cleanly when run alone — so
# the obvious check says "flake" and the gap survives. Both shapes are read now.
#
# The fifth, closed by SONNY-323: this script read one test framework's log shapes, and this
# repository has had two halves since SONNY-126. A server-half battery runs correctly — the mutants
# apply, vitest really runs, the tree is restored — and then every kill it found is reported "KILLED
# by the compiler — the suite never ran ... No test evidence: this says the mutant does not build,
# not that it is covered". The verdict and the count are right and the sentence beside them is
# false: SONNY-131 reports nineteen mutants that way and SONNY-133 twelve, every one of them killed
# by named tests its own log lists plainly, including the one that would have let a client's retry
# bill twice. Two things follow, and the second is why this is numbered beside the other routes
# rather than filed as a wording defect. A reader who believes the report concludes the battery
# proved nothing about coverage. And with no failure ever classified, mutate-untrusted-failures
# cannot reach that half at all — so a flaky vitest test manufactures a kill there exactly the way
# PR #109's R9 did before the third route was closed, and UNATTRIBUTED can never be reported.
# Both formats are read now, chosen by what the log looks like rather than by a flag.
#
# Exit codes
#   0  the battery ran and every mutant was killed
#   2  the battery ran and at least one mutant SURVIVED, or came back UNATTRIBUTED — both are
#      findings rather than errors: the first says nothing holds the behaviour, the second says
#      this run cannot tell whether anything does
#   1  the battery did not run, or its result could not be trusted: a dirty tree, another battery
#      already holding this checkout, a suite command that would build into the shared .build/, an
#      unparseable plan, a mutant whose `from` text does not match exactly once, a missing or empty
#      mutate-untrusted-failures, a red baseline, or a tree left dirty by the suite itself

set -euo pipefail

SELF="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
readonly SELF
readonly SCRIPT_NAME="scripts/mutate"

# Which failures carry no information about a mutant. Beside this script rather than inside it: it
# is edited far more often than the harness is, and a reader asking why a kill was not counted
# should not have to read bash to find the answer. Its own header has the format and the argument.
UNTRUSTED_FILE="$(dirname "$SELF")/mutate-untrusted-failures"
readonly UNTRUSTED_FILE

# The exact flagged test command from CLAUDE.md, plus two flags that are only isolation. Plain
# `swift test` never gets as far as running here — CLAUDE.md's Commands section has which flag
# fixes which failure — and the flagged command's own CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH and default build
# directory both sit in .build/, which every other build in this checkout also writes. Single quotes are deliberate — $MUTATE_SCRATCH is expanded by the subshell that runs
# the command, not now.
#
# Isolation does not change what is measured, which was checked rather than assumed: at c3b8166
# the flagged command and this one each reported 1410 tests in 111 suites, and a cold build of
# each carried the same six compiler-warning sites.
readonly DEFAULT_TEST_CMD='env CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH="$MUTATE_SCRATCH/clang-module-cache" swift test --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" --disable-sandbox -Xswiftc -F -Xswiftc /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/Frameworks -Xlinker -rpath -Xlinker /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/Frameworks -Xlinker -rpath -Xlinker /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/usr/lib'

TEST_CMD="${MUTATE_TEST_CMD:-$DEFAULT_TEST_CMD}"
readonly TEST_CMD

WORKDIR=""

# The file currently carrying a mutant, and the byte-exact copy taken before it was applied. Set
# for exactly as long as the tree is mutated, so an interrupted run — Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, a die()
# from anywhere — puts the file back instead of leaving a mutant behind for someone to commit.
ACTIVE_FILE=""
ACTIVE_BACKUP=""

# The run lock. LOCK_DIR is set as soon as this process knows where the lock lives — including in a
# process the lock REFUSED — so LOCK_OWNED, not LOCK_DIR, is what release_lock tests. A refused
# second invocation must never remove the lock the first one is still holding.
LOCK_DIR=""
LOCK_OWNED=""

# The build directory this battery compiles into, and the one-line description of it the report
# prints. Both are set by require_isolated_test_command, which is also what refuses a replacement
# suite command that would build into the shared .build/ instead.
MUTATE_SCRATCH=""
TEST_CMD_ISOLATION=""

# Set only by finish(), which is the one way this script is allowed to end. Everything else —
# `set -u` on an unbound variable, `set -e` on a failed command, a syntax error on a path never
# taken — reaches the EXIT trap with this still empty, and assert_intended_exit turns that into a
# non-zero exit.
#
# It exists because on bash 3.2, the shell this actually runs under, **the presence of an EXIT trap
# makes a `set -u` death exit 0**. Measured three ways: the same death with no trap exits 1; with a
# trap it exits 0; and the usual remedy, a trap doing `rc=$?; trap - EXIT; exit $rc`, still exits 0,
# because $? is already 0 by the time the trap runs. There is nothing to preserve — the status has
# to be manufactured, so a legitimate exit has to announce itself. (PR #70 review, F6. The earlier
# fix for this symptom changed the battery() call site instead, which governs a real but different
# hazard — see the note there — and left this one live.)
MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED=""

finish() {
  MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED="yes"
  exit "$1"
}

# Defined above cleanup on purpose: cleanup runs from the EXIT trap, which is armed near the top
# of this file, and a trap that calls a function defined further down would fail rather than clean
# up if the harness ever died in between.
release_lock() {
  if [[ -n "$LOCK_OWNED" && -n "$LOCK_DIR" && -d "$LOCK_DIR" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  fi
  LOCK_OWNED=""
}

cleanup() {
  if [[ -n "$ACTIVE_FILE" && -f "$ACTIVE_BACKUP" ]]; then
    cp "$ACTIVE_BACKUP" "$ACTIVE_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true
    printf '%s: interrupted mid-mutant — restored %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$ACTIVE_FILE" >&2
    ACTIVE_FILE=""
  fi
  if [[ -n "$WORKDIR" && -d "$WORKDIR" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$WORKDIR"
    WORKDIR=""
  fi
  # After the restore above, never before it: the lock is what keeps a second battery out, and the
  # tree has to be back to what HEAD says before that second battery can possibly start.
  release_lock
}

# Runs last in every EXIT trap, after the cleanup it must not pre-empt.
assert_intended_exit() {
  if [[ -z "$MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED" ]]; then
    printf '%s: exiting 1 — the harness ended at a point that is not one of its exits.\n' \
      "$SCRIPT_NAME" >&2
    printf '%s: any output above this line is not a battery result. See the error above it.\n' \
      "$SCRIPT_NAME" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

trap 'cleanup; assert_intended_exit' EXIT
trap 'finish 130' INT TERM

die() {
  printf '%s: %s\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$*" >&2
  finish 1
}

require_command() {
  command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "required command not found: $1"
}

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
Usage: scripts/mutate <plan> [--only ID[,ID...]] [--check]
       scripts/mutate selftest
       scripts/mutate unlock
       scripts/mutate --help

Runs a mutation battery against the COMMITTED tree: for each mutant it replaces an exact block
of text, runs the test suite, records which tests failed, and restores the file byte for byte.

It refuses to start while the working tree is dirty, and it refuses to start beside another
battery in the same checkout. Those two refusals are the point of the script, and no flag turns
either of them off.

Plan format — one record per mutant, directives at column 0:

    # a comment; blank lines are ignored too
    >>> mutant M1 the pending-approval guard removed
    >>> file Sources/MacAgentCore/AgentViewModel.swift
    >>> from
            guard !isAwaitingApproval else { return }
    >>> to
    >>> end

    >>> mutant M2 the morning boundary widened by an hour
    >>> file Sources/MacAgent/CommandCenterView.swift
    >>> from
            case 5..<12: period = "Morning"
    >>> to
            case 5..<13: period = "Morning"
    >>> end

  >>> mutant <id> <note>   The id is the first word and must be unique; the rest is a note,
                           echoed in the report so the summary reads like the changelog entry
                           it will become.
  >>> file <path>          Repository-root-relative.
  >>> from ... >>> to      The exact lines to replace, copied verbatim out of the file,
                           indentation included. Matched as literal bytes — never as a regex.
  >>> to ... >>> end       The lines to put in their place. An empty block deletes them.

The `from` text must occur EXACTLY ONCE in the file. Zero matches or two abort the whole run
before a single test executes: a mutant that did not apply produces a green suite that reads
exactly like a passing battery, which is how one round here reported four mis-patched mutants
as clean runs.

Keep the plan file outside the working tree. A plan committed nowhere is an untracked file, and
an untracked file is a dirty tree — the harness will refuse its own plan.

Concurrency, and the build directory:
  A battery holds a run lock for its whole duration, in .git/ where it cannot dirty the tree, so a
  second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout is refused instead of interleaving with the first. It
  also builds into a scratch directory of its own rather than the shared .build/, so an ordinary
  `swift build` from another terminal cannot corrupt what this run measures.

  `scripts/mutate unlock` clears a lock left behind by a killed run. It refuses while the owning
  process is still alive, so it cannot break a live battery — and once that process is gone, the
  same command clears what it left.

  The first battery in a checkout pays for one cold build of that private scratch directory
  (about a minute and a half in this repository). Every battery after it reuses the directory and
  builds incrementally, so the cost is paid once, not per run.

What this does and does not prevent:
  Prevented: a second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout; and any other build in this checkout
  corrupting this run's compiled artifacts, its llbuild database, or its clang module cache.

  Not prevented, and worth knowing rather than assuming otherwise:

    * Nothing here stops another terminal from running `swift build` mid-battery. That build can
      no longer hurt the battery, but the battery still hurts it: it compiles whatever is on disk
      at that moment, which during a run includes a mutant.
    * A process that WRITES a tracked file during a mutant's suite run is caught after that
      mutant, by the dirty-tree check between mutants, not while it runs. A `git checkout` in this
      checkout mid-build is the loud version and usually breaks the build outright with "input
      file ... was modified during the build".
    * SwiftPM's caches under ~ (dependency clones, the manifest cache) are shared by every build
      on this machine and are outside a script's reach.
    * A FLAKY TEST READS AS A KILL, unless its failure is declared. A mutant is judged killed by
      whether the suite failed, so a test that fails for a timing reason of its own is
      indistinguishable from one that caught the mutant — and it fails in the reassuring direction,
      recording a guard that does not exist. A battery puts more sustained load on the machine than
      an ordinary run, which is exactly the condition a timing-sensitive test needs to fail; two
      mic-hover-hint tests were found this way (SONNY-199, 2 failures in 12 full-suite runs on
      PR #89, since fixed) and PR #109's R9 was reported killed by a third (SONNY-224).
      What is prevented now: a failure matching a signature in mutate-untrusted-failures is
      excluded from the kill count, and a mutant with nothing else against it is reported
      UNATTRIBUTED rather than KILLED. The match is on the issue a test recorded, not on the test's
      name, and it is per issue rather than per test — a test is excused only when EVERY issue it
      recorded is declared, so the same test still counts when it fails on a real assertion beside
      a backstop. Two seams are left in that. Tests sharing a function name across suites merge,
      because neither line the classifier reads names a suite; the merge undercounts kills and can
      print the wrong one of the two names, never the reverse. And a mutant that TRAPS the test
      process names no test at all, so nothing here applies to it — that is the case CLAUDE.md's
      `try #require` gotcha is about.
      A third seam was found and closed rather than left here (SONNY-305). A suite can run to
      completion, record every one of a test's issues, and never print the per-test summary line
      that names it — measured on one mutant at one tree, absent inside a battery and present on
      the same mutant run alone. The classifier read only that summary line, so the kill was
      reported as "named no test", pointing at a crash that had not happened; both line shapes are
      read now, and the trap case above still reports exactly that, correctly, because a dead
      process prints no issue lines either. The two causes no longer share one message. That seam
      also had a direction that manufactured a kill: a run whose only red was a DECLARED failure
      left the classifier with nothing to report, so it was counted KILLED rather than
      UNATTRIBUTED — everything in this bullet, defeated by the absence of one line.
      UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests is exempt from all of it, by name: it is the guard on the
      declarations, so letting the declarations excuse its failure would leave a stale file marking
      itself trustworthy. That happened, and the battery printed a reassuring line while running
      against a file it had just been told was wrong.
      What is NOT prevented, and this is the part to keep in mind: that file is a list, and no
      mechanism can tell it what it is missing. A timing-sensitive test whose failure matches no
      signature reads as a kill exactly as before. `UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests` in the Swift
      suite holds the direction that can be checked — a declaration whose source stops appearing at
      the number of sites it declares fails the suite, so rewording one of six copies of a backstop
      is caught rather than leaving that copy silently undeclared — and the other direction is
      judgment. So the old advice stands and is no longer the only thing standing: read the names,
      not the count. A mutant killed only by a test with no plausible connection to it is a flake
      wearing a kill's clothes.
      A flaky BASELINE was the harmless direction by comparison — it aborted the run, which cost a
      rerun and told no lies. It now aborts only when something real is red; a baseline red purely
      on declared failures prints them and continues, because none of them can manufacture a kill.
    * TWO TEST FRAMEWORKS ARE READ, AND ONLY TWO. swift-testing's console output, for the app half
      under Sources/ and Tests/, and vitest's, for the server half under server/ — chosen by what
      the log looks like rather than by a flag, so a plan never has to declare which half it is
      (SONNY-323). What is read of vitest is its error summary (the ` FAIL  <file> > <suite> >
      <test>` entries and the message under each) and its `Tests` line; the default reporter and
      `--reporter=dot` both print those, measured on this repository's server suite. A suite command
      whose output carries neither framework's shapes is not refused — it is reported as a run that
      named no test, which is the same thing this harness says about a mutant that trapped the test
      process, and it names the log to read.
    * The lock is per-worktree, deliberately: each worktree has its own .build/, so two worktrees
      running batteries at once share nothing and are not refused.
    * MUTATE_TEST_CMD replaces the suite command outright. If the replacement invokes SwiftPM it
      must carry --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" or the run is refused; if it invokes something
      else, this script cannot know what that something else shares.

Environment:
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD   Replaces the suite command. Add `--filter <Suite>` through it for a fast
                    re-run of one mutant, keeping --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" in it. The
                    report always prints the command it actually ran, so a pasted log can never
                    misrepresent what was measured.
  MUTATE_SCRATCH    Exported by the harness, not set by you: the build directory private to this
                    run. A replacement suite command that builds with SwiftPM has to use it.

Output:
  Per-mutant logs are written under .build/mutate/<sha>-<timestamp>/, because .build/ is
  gitignored here and the harness must not dirty the tree it just insisted on. That is checked,
  not assumed: a repository that does not ignore .build/ gets its logs in a temp directory.
USAGE
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The guard. Everything else in this file is convenience.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `-c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal`, not a bare `git status`: that config is honoured by
# --porcelain, so `git config status.showUntrackedFiles no` deletes the untracked half of this
# guard without anyone editing the script — an effective override in a script whose stated property
# is that it has none. (PR #70 review, F1.)
require_clean_tree() {
  local dirty
  dirty="$(git -c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal status --porcelain)"
  if [[ -z "$dirty" ]]; then
    return 0
  fi
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — the working tree is not clean.

$dirty

A battery's result is only meaningful if it can be attributed to a commit, and its revert step is
only safe if what it reverts to is what is committed. Uncommitted work makes both untrue: the run
measures a tree no SHA names, and any recovery by \`git checkout\` — which is how every battery in
this repository used to revert — deletes the work instead of the mutation. That has happened five
times, and three of those produced false measurements: once a bogus kill, which is the dangerous
direction because it claims coverage that is not there, and twice bogus survivors against code
that had just been deleted.

Commit or stash the paths above, then re-run. There is no override flag.
EOF
  finish 1
}

require_clean_tree_after() {
  local context="$1"
  local dirty
  dirty="$(git -c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal status --porcelain)"
  if [[ -z "$dirty" ]]; then
    return 0
  fi
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: aborting — the tree is dirty after $context.

$dirty

The battery stops here rather than carrying an unexplained change into the next mutant, which
would make every result after this one describe a tree nobody chose. Restore the paths above by
hand and work out what wrote them before re-running.
EOF
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second guard: one battery at a time, in a build directory of its own.
#
# The lock lives inside .git/, which `git status` never reports, so taking it cannot dirty the tree
# the next check is about to insist is clean. .git/ is also per-worktree — `git rev-parse
# --absolute-git-dir` answers .git/worktrees/<name> inside a linked worktree — and per-worktree is
# exactly the right scope, because each worktree has its own .build/ and two of them running
# batteries at once share nothing.
#
# `mkdir` is the whole atomicity argument: on any POSIX filesystem it either creates the directory
# or fails because it exists, in one step, with no window between the test and the create.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

lock_field() {
  [[ -f "$LOCK_DIR/owner" ]] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$1 //p" "$LOCK_DIR/owner" | head -1
}

# Prints why the lock is stale, or prints nothing at all if its owner is genuinely still running a
# battery. Both halves of a pid check matter here: a pid that no longer exists is the ordinary
# killed-run case, and a pid that exists but belongs to something else is pid reuse, which on a Mac
# that has wrapped around its pid space is not exotic. The one case this reads as live when it is
# not is a pid reused by anything whose command line happens to say "mutate" — another battery, or
# an editor with this file open. That error refuses a run rather than allowing two, and
# `scripts/mutate unlock` reports the same reading, so the way out of it is visible.
lock_stale_reason() {
  local pid="$1" owner_command
  if [[ ! -f "$LOCK_DIR/owner" ]]; then
    printf 'it carries no owner record, so whatever made it died between the mkdir and the write'
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ ! "$pid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    printf 'its owner record names no usable pid'
    return 0
  fi
  owner_command="$(ps -p "$pid" -o command= 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [[ -z "$owner_command" ]]; then
    printf 'the process that took it (pid %s) is gone' "$pid"
    return 0
  fi
  case "$owner_command" in
    *mutate*) return 0 ;;
  esac
  printf 'pid %s belongs to something else now: %s' "$pid" "$owner_command"
}

# A live lock held by the OTHER measurement tool in this checkout. The two tools take separate
# locks — .git/warnings.lock and .git/mutate.lock — and until SONNY-184(c) neither knew the other
# existed, so a warning count could be taken during a battery: mid-mutant by
# construction, a deliberately broken tree, reported as the tree's own and stamped with a SHA that
# never contained that code. Same class of false measurement SONNY-176 closed for two batteries
# sharing .build/.
#
# Reads the owner record both scripts write, and applies the same liveness test as
# lock_stale_reason, so a lock a killed run left behind blocks nothing: this refuses only for a pid
# that still exists and whose command line names the other tool. It is deliberately per-checkout —
# a battery in a different worktree has its own working tree and its own build directory and is no
# hazard at all.
foreign_lock_holder() {
  local dir="$1" pattern="$2" pid owner_command
  [[ -d "$dir" && -f "$dir/owner" ]] || return 1
  pid="$(sed -n 's/^pid //p' "$dir/owner" | head -1)"
  [[ "$pid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
  owner_command="$(ps -p "$pid" -o command= 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [[ -n "$owner_command" ]] || return 1
  case "$owner_command" in
    $pattern) printf '%s' "$pid"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

foreign_lock_field() {
  local dir="$1" key="$2"
  [[ -f "$dir/owner" ]] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$key //p" "$dir/owner" | head -1
}

refuse_warnings_run() {
  local pid="$1" dir="$2"
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — a scripts/warnings run holds this checkout.

  pid     : $pid
  started : $(foreign_lock_field "$dir" started)
  head    : $(foreign_lock_field "$dir" head)
  lock    : $dir

That script compiles this whole tree to count its warnings, and this battery would start editing
source files underneath that build. Its count would then describe a tree carrying a mutant, stamped
with a SHA that never contained one — and this battery would be measuring artifacts a second build
is writing beside it. Neither report could show it.

Wait for that run to finish. If it is already gone, \`scripts/warnings unlock\` clears its lock.
EOF
  finish 1
}

refuse_concurrent_run() {
  local pid="$1"
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — another battery already holds this checkout.

  pid     : ${pid:-unknown}
  started : $(lock_field started)
  plan    : $(lock_field plan)
  head    : $(lock_field head)
  lock    : $LOCK_DIR

Two batteries in one checkout share the working tree and the build directory. The tree already
carries the other run's mutant, and both runs compile out of the same directory, so each one is
measuring a tree and a set of artifacts the other is changing underneath it. Nothing in either
report could show that: a contaminated battery prints the same counts a clean one does, and the
comfortable direction to be wrong in is a false KILL, which claims coverage that is not there.

Wait for that run to finish. If it is already gone, \`$SCRIPT_NAME unlock\` clears the lock — it
refuses while the owner is alive, so it cannot break a live run.
EOF
  finish 1
}

acquire_lock() {
  local plan="$1" git_dir attempt reason owner_pid
  git_dir="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null)" \
    || die "not inside a git repository — there is nowhere to hold the run lock"
  LOCK_DIR="$git_dir/mutate.lock"

  local warnings_pid
  if warnings_pid="$(foreign_lock_holder "$git_dir/warnings.lock" '*warnings*')"; then
    refuse_warnings_run "$warnings_pid" "$git_dir/warnings.lock"
  fi

  for attempt in 1 2 3; do
    if mkdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
      LOCK_OWNED="yes"
      {
        printf 'pid %s\n' "$$"
        printf 'started %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
        printf 'plan %s\n' "$plan"
        printf 'head %s\n' "$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown')"
      } >"$LOCK_DIR/owner"
      return 0
    fi

    owner_pid="$(lock_field pid)"
    reason="$(lock_stale_reason "$owner_pid")"
    [[ -n "$reason" ]] || refuse_concurrent_run "$owner_pid"
    printf '%s: cleared a stale lock — %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$reason" >&2
    rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  done

  die "could not take the run lock at $LOCK_DIR — it is being created and cleared underneath this
       run. Something else is starting batteries in a loop; stop that before measuring anything."
}

# `scripts/mutate unlock`. Every state a lock can be left in has a way out that is not a hand-edit:
# gone owner or unreadable record, cleared here; live owner, refused here and cleared by this same
# command once that process ends, however it ends.
unlock_command() {
  require_command git
  local root git_dir pid reason
  root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  cd "$root"
  git_dir="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  LOCK_DIR="$git_dir/mutate.lock"

  if [[ ! -d "$LOCK_DIR" ]]; then
    printf '%s: no run lock is held in this checkout.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
    printf '    looked at %s\n' "$LOCK_DIR"
    finish 0
  fi

  pid="$(lock_field pid)"
  reason="$(lock_stale_reason "$pid")"
  if [[ -z "$reason" ]]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to clear the lock — pid $pid is still running a battery.

  started : $(lock_field started)
  plan    : $(lock_field plan)
  head    : $(lock_field head)

Clearing it now would let a second battery start beside that one, which is the measurement this
lock exists to prevent. End that run first (\`kill $pid\`) and its own trap releases the lock on the
way out. If it was killed with -9 and left this behind, run this command again — with the process
gone the lock is stale and this clears it.
EOF
    finish 1
  fi

  rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  printf '%s: cleared a stale lock — %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$reason"
  finish 0
}

# Where this battery builds. Same shape and the same reason as the log directory below: .build/ is
# gitignored here, so a scratch directory inside it cannot dirty the tree, and a repository that
# does not ignore .build/ gets one outside the working tree instead.
mutate_scratch_path() {
  if git check-ignore -q ".build/mutate-scratch" 2>/dev/null; then
    printf '%s/.build/mutate-scratch' "$1"
  else
    printf '%s/scratch' "$WORKDIR"
  fi
}

# MUTATE_TEST_CMD replaces the suite command outright, which means it can replace the isolation
# with it. A replacement that invokes SwiftPM without a scratch path builds into the shared .build/
# — the exact hazard this file just spent two mechanisms on — so it is refused rather than warned
# about, because a warning in a report nobody re-reads is the non-mechanism this repository keeps
# rejecting. A replacement that is not a SwiftPM invocation at all has no build directory to share;
# the selftest's stand-ins are all of that kind, and the report says so rather than claiming an
# isolation that means nothing there.
#
# The word test is deliberately narrow: `swift` followed by whitespace and not preceded by a word
# character or a dot. `-Xswiftc` and `sample.swift` are both `swift` inside a longer token and
# neither is a SwiftPM invocation.
require_isolated_test_command() {
  case " $TEST_CMD " in
    *[!A-Za-z0-9_.]swift[[:space:]]*) ;;
    *)
      TEST_CMD_ISOLATION="not a SwiftPM invocation — it has no build directory to share"
      return 0
      ;;
  esac
  case "$TEST_CMD" in
    *--scratch-path*|*MUTATE_SCRATCH*)
      TEST_CMD_ISOLATION="$MUTATE_SCRATCH (this run's own, not the shared .build/)"
      return 0
      ;;
  esac
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — MUTATE_TEST_CMD builds with SwiftPM and names no scratch path.

  $TEST_CMD

That builds into the same .build/ every other build in this checkout writes, and a shared build
directory is how a battery ends up reporting a result nothing in its output can show is wrong.
This run's own directory is exported as \$MUTATE_SCRATCH — put it in the command:

  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='swift test --scratch-path "\$MUTATE_SCRATCH" --filter SomeTests'

Single-quote it, so \$MUTATE_SCRATCH expands when the command runs rather than when you set it.
EOF
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plan parsing. Parallel arrays; index i is one mutant. The macOS system bash is 3.2, so no
# associative arrays. `from`/`to` blocks are held as files, not variables, because they are
# matched as raw bytes and a shell variable cannot hold a trailing newline honestly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

M_COUNT=0
M_ID=()
M_FILE=()
M_NOTE=()

parse_plan() {
  local plan="$1"
  [[ -f "$plan" ]] || die "plan file not found: $plan"

  local state="top" lineno=0 line rest existing i
  local id="" note="" file=""

  while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n "$line" ]]; do
    lineno=$((lineno + 1))
    case "$state" in
      top)
        if [[ -z "${line//[[:space:]]/}" || "$line" == '#'* ]]; then
          continue
        fi
        [[ "$line" == '>>> mutant '* ]] \
          || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> mutant <id> <note>', got: $line"
        rest="${line#>>> mutant }"
        id="${rest%%[[:space:]]*}"
        [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$ ]] \
          || die "$plan:$lineno: '$id' is not a usable mutant id (letters, digits, . _ - only)"
        i=0
        while [[ $i -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
          existing="${M_ID[$i]}"
          [[ "$existing" != "$id" ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: duplicate mutant id: $id"
          i=$((i + 1))
        done
        note="${rest#"$id"}"
        note="${note#"${note%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
        state="want-file"
        ;;
      want-file)
        [[ "$line" == '>>> file '* ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> file <path>', got: $line"
        file="${line#>>> file }"
        [[ -n "$file" ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: '>>> file' names no path"
        state="want-from"
        ;;
      want-from)
        [[ "$line" == '>>> from' ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> from', got: $line"
        : >"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from"
        state="in-from"
        ;;
      in-from)
        if [[ "$line" == '>>> to' ]]; then
          : >"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.to"
          state="in-to"
        else
          printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from"
        fi
        ;;
      in-to)
        if [[ "$line" == '>>> end' ]]; then
          [[ -s "$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from" ]] \
            || die "$plan:$lineno: mutant $id has an empty '>>> from' block"
          M_ID[$M_COUNT]="$id"
          M_FILE[$M_COUNT]="$file"
          M_NOTE[$M_COUNT]="$note"
          M_COUNT=$((M_COUNT + 1))
          state="top"
        else
          printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.to"
        fi
        ;;
    esac
  done <"$plan"

  [[ "$state" == "top" ]] \
    || die "$plan: unexpected end of file — mutant '$id' is missing its '>>> end'"
  [[ $M_COUNT -gt 0 ]] || die "$plan: contains no mutants"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Literal byte matching and patching. python3 in binary mode, deliberately: a battery here once
# used `perl -0pi` and corrupted UTF-8 punctuation across a file's doc comments, mutating far more
# than it meant to. Reading and writing bytes cannot re-encode anything it did not touch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

occurrences() {
  python3 - "$1" "$2" <<'PY'
import pathlib, sys
haystack = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_bytes()
needle = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).read_bytes()
print(haystack.count(needle))
PY
}

apply_mutant() {
  python3 - "$1" "$2" "$3" <<'PY'
import pathlib, sys
target = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])
data = target.read_bytes()
needle = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).read_bytes()
replacement = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[3]).read_bytes()
if data.count(needle) != 1:
    sys.stderr.write("match count changed between pre-flight and apply\n")
    sys.exit(1)
target.write_bytes(data.replace(needle, replacement, 1))
PY
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running and reading the suite. swift-testing's console glyphs vary with the terminal, so no
# pattern below matches a particular icon. One of them depends on a weaker property that holds for
# every set: `classify_failures`' boundary rule requires only that whatever prefix a line carries
# has no letters in it, which is true of the SF Symbols set, of the unicode and ASCII fallbacks,
# and of a line with no prefix at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

run_suite() {
  local log="$1"
  local code=0
  bash -c "$TEST_CMD" >"$log" 2>&1 || code=$?
  return "$code"
}

# Did any test run at all, or did the tree not even build? The whole "KILLED by the compiler"
# branch hangs off this one question, and the two halves of this repository answer it on different
# lines — swift-testing under Sources/ and Tests/, vitest under server/ (SONNY-323).
#
# swift-testing prints "Test run started." before it loads anything, so its absence means the
# compile failed. vitest prints its own banner just as early, and then — the part a single tell
# would miss — prints "Tests  no tests" when every test file failed to transform, which is the same
# condition arriving through a different door: the mutant did not build, and nothing ran.
suite_started() {
  if grep -q 'Test run started\.' "$1"; then
    return 0
  fi
  if ! vitest_started "$1"; then
    return 1
  fi
  ! vitest_ran_no_tests "$1"
}

# vitest's own two tells, measured on this repository's server suite rather than written from
# memory. `npx vitest run test/screen.test.ts` with src/model/limits.ts made merely wrong prints
# " RUN  v4.1.11 <dir>" and "      Tests  1 failed | 25 passed (26)"; with the same file made
# unparseable it prints the same banner, a "Failed Suites" heading, " FAIL  test/screen.test.ts
# [ test/screen.test.ts ]" and "      Tests  no tests". Both halves are load-bearing. Without the
# banner check a command that died before vitest ever started — `npm run build && npx vitest run`
# with the build red — would read as a suite that ran. Without the "no tests" check a mutant that
# breaks the parse would be reported killed by a "test" named `test/x.test.ts [ test/x.test.ts ]`,
# which is a file that failed to load wearing a test's name.
vitest_started() {
  grep -q '^[[:space:]]*RUN[[:space:]][[:space:]]*v[0-9]' "$1"
}

vitest_ran_no_tests() {
  grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*Tests[[:space:]]+no tests([[:space:]]|$)' "$1"
}

run_tally() {
  local tally
  # -E, not BRE: BSD sed has no \| alternation and would silently print nothing.
  tally="$(sed -nE 's/^.*Test run with (.*) (passed|failed) after .*$/\1/p' "$1" | tail -1)"
  if [[ -n "$tally" ]]; then
    printf '%s' "$tally"
    return 0
  fi
  # vitest's summary line instead: "      Tests  26 passed (26)". Reached only when the swift form
  # matched nothing, so a swift log can never be read by this second pattern.
  sed -nE 's/^[[:space:]]*Tests[[:space:]]+([^[:space:]].*)$/\1/p' "$1" | tail -1
}

# One line per failing test: "Test <name> failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue." A
# parameterized test reads "<name> with 10 test cases failed after ...", so the case count is
# trimmed off the end. The run-level line ("Test run with N tests ... failed") is excluded by
# name, and suite-level lines never contain " Test ".
#
# `sed '/^run with /d'` and not `grep -v '^run with '`, for a reason that has nothing to do with
# style: grep exits 1 when it matches nothing, and on EMPTY input it always matches nothing. Under
# `set -o pipefail` that made this whole function fail whenever no test was named — which is exactly
# the case a mutant that TRAPS the test process produces, since a trapped process prints no
# "Test ... failed after" line at all. The caller's `names="$(failing_tests "$log")"` then failed
# under `set -e`, and the battery died three lines above the arm written to report it: "exiting 1 —
# the harness ended at a point that is not one of its exits", with nothing above it saying which
# mutant or why. sed deletes lines and exits 0 on empty input, so the arm is reachable and a trapped
# run is reported instead of ending the battery mid-sentence (SONNY-224, founder decision after
# PR #112's review recorded it).
# When that line is absent for every failing test, the issue lines are read instead — see the
# comment below (SONNY-305). A vitest log has neither shape and falls through the same way, which is
# how the server half is named at all: the fallback delegates to `classify_failures`, and that is
# where both frameworks are read (SONNY-323).
failing_tests() {
  local named
  named="$(sed -n 's/^.*[[:space:]]Test \(.*\) failed after .*$/\1/p' "$1" \
    | sed '/^run with /d' \
    | sed 's/ with [0-9][0-9]* test cases$//' \
    | sort -u)"
  if [[ -n "$named" ]]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$named"
    return 0
  fi

  # Nothing named itself on the summary line. That is two different situations sharing one silence,
  # and until SONNY-305 this function answered "no tests" to both. A mutant that TRAPS the test
  # process really does name nothing — a dead process prints no issue lines either. But a suite
  # that ran to completion can record every one of a test's issues and never print the per-test
  # summary line that names it, and then the log names the killing test repeatedly, on a line shape
  # nothing here was reading. Measured on the same mutant at the same tree, once inside a battery
  # and once alone: five identical `recorded an issue` lines both times, the summary line only the
  # second time, and only the second run could say which test caught it.
  #
  # The fallback delegates to classify_failures rather than adding a second regex here, and that is
  # not tidiness. Two engines reading one line shape can disagree — sed is greedy and the
  # classifier's pattern is not — and a disagreement lands in the worst possible place: the caller
  # gates on THIS function and then attributes with THAT one, so names-here-but-nothing-there turns
  # a correctly counted kill into an UNATTRIBUTED with an empty evidence list. One reader, one
  # answer, and the two cannot drift apart.
  #
  # classify_failures needs $UNTRUSTED_FILE, which require_untrusted_declarations has already
  # insisted on by the time any log exists; both call sites are inside battery(), after it.
  classify_failures "$1" | awk -F'\t' 'NF > 1 { print $2 }' | sort -u
}

# A battery is only sound while every way the suite can go red is a way the mutant caused, and it
# is not: a test that fails because the machine was busy fails identically with a mutant present and
# absent. `mutate-untrusted-failures` is where that gets declared, and this is what reads it.
#
# One line per failing test, tab-separated:
#   T<TAB><name>                    the failure is evidence — an ordinary assertion went red
#   U<TAB><name><TAB><signature>    every issue it recorded was declared, so none of it is
#
# Classification is per ISSUE, not per test. That is the property mutate-untrusted-failures argues
# for in its own header — "the same test can fail two ways ... matching the message keeps the first
# and drops the second" — and the first version of this function did not have it: it ORed over a
# test's issues, so one declared backstop threw away a real assertion the same test had recorded
# beside it, which is precisely what that header says a name list would do. A test is untrusted only
# when EVERY issue it recorded is declared; one undeclared issue makes it evidence again. It lands
# on the most plausible mutant class, too: one that hangs a view model fires the backstop and breaks
# that same test's assertions (PR #112 review, F2).
#
# Python rather than another sed, for a reason worth stating: swift-testing prints an issue as one
# line naming the test and a headline, then the issue's own comment on continuation lines below it.
# The text a signature needs to match is usually on those continuation lines — `Issue.record` puts
# only "Issue recorded" on the named line — so the block has to be reassembled before it can be
# matched, which a line-at-a-time grep cannot do.
classify_failures() {
  python3 - "$1" "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" <<'PY'
import re
import sys

log_path, decl_path = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]

# The failures no declaration may excuse, because they are the guard ON the declarations. When
# `UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests` goes red it is saying the file this classifier is reading has
# gone stale — and excusing that with the stale file is circular. It was not hypothetical: the
# guard's failure message quoted the very signature it had caught going missing, this classifier
# matched it, baseline_trusted came back empty, and the battery printed "BASELINE RED, and only on
# failures this harness does not trust ... Continuing" and ran the whole run against a declaration
# file it had just been told was wrong (PR #112 review, F1, reproduced by rewording one source).
#
# Named rather than message-matched, and the distinction is the point: everything in
# mutate-untrusted-failures says "this failure is not evidence about a mutant", while this says
# "this failure invalidates the file that decides what is". A name is the right key for that, and
# the staleness a name list normally suffers is covered — `UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests` asserts
# this set is exactly its own `@Test` functions, so a rename fails the suite rather than quietly
# emptying the list.
ALWAYS_TRUSTED = frozenset({
    "theDeclarationFileParsesAndEveryRecordIsComplete",
    "everyTestTreeThisScanReadsIsReadToSomething",
    "everyDeclaredSourceStillAppearsAtTheSiteCountItDeclares",
    "theSiteCountCheckRefusesARecordThatDeclaresNoSites",
    "theHarnessAlwaysTrustsExactlyTheTestsInThisSuite",
    "noDeclaredSignatureAppearsInThisSuitesOwnSource",
})

signatures = []
with open(decl_path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
    for line in handle:
        if line.startswith(">>> signature "):
            text = line[len(">>> signature "):].strip()
            if text:
                signatures.append(text)

# `.*?` between the two fixed halves because a parameterized test's issue line is not the plain
# form: it reads `recorded an issue with 1 argument mode → .safe at File.swift:9:9:`, and a pattern
# written for `recorded an issue at` misses every case of every parameterized test — which lands
# them all in the trusted column, silently, in exactly the direction this file exists to close.
# Found by running this over a real battery log rather than by reading it (SONNY-224); the
# parameterized case in `selftest` is what keeps it found.
issue_re = re.compile(r"Test (.+?) recorded an issue\b.*? at \S+:\d+:\d+: (.*)$")

# What ends an issue's block. Anchored, and after a prefix carrying no letters, because that is the
# shape of every line swift-testing writes and of no line an issue's message can produce: the
# console recorder puts a symbol and two spaces in front of its own lines, and whichever symbol set
# a terminal gets — SF Symbols glyphs here, the unicode or ASCII fallbacks elsewhere — none of them
# is a letter. A verb is required as well, so that even a message opening on punctuation cannot
# masquerade as a record line.
#
# The pattern was `(?:^|\s)(?:Test|Suite) \S`, which fires on those two words ANYWHERE in a line,
# an issue's own message included. An issue whose first message line read "waited on Suite
# ProductShellTests to quiesce" ended its own block there, and every line after it — the declared
# signature among them — was dropped, so the mutant came back KILLED: a manufactured kill, which is
# the one outcome this harness exists to prevent (PR #112 review, F3). Latent when it was found,
# since all five declared messages happen to render on one line, and nothing would have detected it.
#
# What is left, stated rather than implied: a *record* line whose verb is not on its own physical
# line no longer ends a block. That happens when a parameterized test's argument contains a newline,
# and the cost is over-capture — an open block absorbs a few lines it should not — which can only
# make a failure look MORE declared than it is. Undercounting a kill is the safe direction; the
# truncation this replaces was the other one.
#
# The verb is `recorded`, not `recorded an issue`, and that one word is SONNY-315 (found in PR #139's
# cycle-2 battery at 157ef03). A KNOWN issue's record line — `Test <name> recorded a known issue at
# File.swift:174:55: Issue recorded`, which swift-testing prints for every `withKnownIssue` — carries
# none of the four old verbs, so it did not end a block. HangBackstopTests emits one on every single
# run by design, and when it landed between a killing test's issue line and that test's failure line,
# its detail lines — a declared signature among them — were read as the KILLING test's own message.
# The mutant came back UNATTRIBUTED: a real kill excused, which is the mirror of the manufactured
# kill this whole mechanism exists to stop, and it understates coverage and sends the reader hunting
# for a test that already exists. It depends on where a parallel suite's output lands rather than on
# what the mutant is, so any mutant can hit it and re-running usually does not: PR #139's battery
# reported it once, then `--only` named the killing test, then a second full battery was clean.
#
# What the widened verb costs, since a boundary that fires too eagerly is PR #112's F3 in the other
# direction: within a line that already begins — after a prefix carrying no letters — with `Test ` or
# `Suite ` and a non-space, the verb set now admits any "recorded". A message line of exactly that
# shape carrying the word "recorded" and none of the other four would end its own block early. That
# is narrower than what it buys, which is every "recorded ..." event kind swift-testing has or grows
# rather than the one wording seen so far.
#
# It does not, and cannot, protect a message SPLIT by an interleaved record line: everything after
# the split is dropped, so a signature there would be lost and the failure would count as evidence.
# What makes that safe in practice is that swift-testing emits an event's lines together — measured
# on a real 2305-test run of this repository's suite, where both multi-line known-issue events came
# out contiguous, and where every interleaving is between events. Nothing here can tell a split
# message from a foreign event, so the choice is which of the two to be wrong about; being wrong
# about the observed one was costing real kills.
boundary_re = re.compile(
    r"^[^A-Za-z]*(?:Test|Suite) \S.*?\b(?:started|passed|failed|skipped|recorded)\b"
)
failed_re = re.compile(r"^.*\sTest (.*) failed after ")

# ── vitest, the server half's suite (SONNY-323) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This repository has had two halves since SONNY-126, and this classifier read one framework's
# shapes — so a server-half battery named no test at all, every kill it found was reported "KILLED
# by the compiler ... No test evidence", and nothing it produced could ever be UNATTRIBUTED. The
# count was right and the sentence beside it was false.
#
# vitest's error summary is the shape worth reading, because it has the same structure
# swift-testing's issue lines do — a line naming the test, then the failure's own message on the
# lines under it — which is why this needs no second classifier, only a second way to open a block:
#
#      FAIL  test/screen.test.ts > the screen route's numbers > derives §6.1's body limit ...
#     AssertionError: expected 3000001 to be 3000000 // Object.is equality
#     ...
#     [a rule of U+23AF and an [n/m] counter, which is what closes the block]
#
# Read instead of the progress section's "× <test name> 3ms" lines, which SONNY-323 suggested and
# which cannot do this job: they carry no message, so nothing there can be matched against a
# declaration and every vitest failure would be evidence by default; and they name the test without
# its file or its describe block, which is the same merge that costs the swift path a name when two
# suites share a function name. The two sections agree on what failed — every failing test appears
# once in each — so reading only the one that carries the evidence loses nothing.
#
# Names are deliberately NOT put through base(). A vitest test name is prose and may contain a
# parenthesis, so splitting on "(" would truncate it and merge tests that differ after it. The swift
# form needs base() because a parameterized test's two log lines disagree about the parameter list;
# vitest prints one line and has no such disagreement.
vitest_fail_re = re.compile(r"^\s*FAIL\s\s+(\S.*?)\s*$")

# An entry counts only INSIDE that section, and this gate is not caution — it is a measurement. A
# failing test's console output is echoed into the log above the section, under a `stdout |
# test/x.test.ts > …` header and then verbatim, so a test that logs a line shaped like an entry
# would otherwise mint a failing test that never existed: evidence out of nothing, which is the
# direction that manufactures a kill. Reproduced on this repository's own server suite with a
# throwaway test that prints ` FAIL  test/made-up.test.ts > a suite > a test that does not exist`
# and then fails; the line arrives at column 0 exactly as an entry does, and only its position
# separates them. (The code frame vitest prints under the real entry quotes that same source line,
# but it arrives prefixed with `      5|` and matches nothing here.)
#
# The section opens on vitest's own heading and closes at the run summary, which is the first line
# after which nothing can be an entry. A log carrying no heading yields no vitest names at all and
# is reported as a run that named no test — the safe direction, and the same thing this harness says
# about a trapped process.
vitest_failures_heading_re = re.compile(r"^\u23af{2,} Failed (?:Tests|Suites)\b")
vitest_summary_re = re.compile(r"^\s*Test Files\s")

# What closes one of those entries. Anchored at the start of the line and requiring the rule itself,
# rather than the weaker "a line carrying no letters" the swift boundary can afford: the blank lines
# inside a failure's own diff would match that, and ending a block early is the direction that drops
# a declared signature and manufactures a kill (PR #112's F3).
vitest_boundary_re = re.compile(r"^\u23af{3,}")


def base(name):
    """The name without its parameter list, which is the only key the two log forms share.

    A parameterized test's issues are printed per case (`aTest(mode: .normal)`) while its failure
    line names the whole test (`aTest(mode:) with 3 test cases`), so nothing else joins them.

    **Two tests with the same function name in different suites merge here, and the log cannot
    separate them** — swift-testing names neither line's suite, so there is nothing else to key on.
    `emptyInputProducesAnEmptyResult` is a `@Test` in four suites today. Per-issue classification is
    what makes that safe in the direction that matters: a merged group counts as evidence when any
    issue in it is undeclared, and at least one of the merged tests really did record that issue, so
    the kill is genuine. What the merge costs is the count and the name — two failing tests are
    reported as one, under whichever name the log listed first (PR #112 review, F2, aggravating).
    """
    return name.split("(", 1)[0]


blocks = {}
order = []
failing = {}

# A test that recorded issues and never got the per-test summary line naming it. Kept separately
# and merged in AFTER the loop, so a log where every failing test does get that line produces the
# same output, in the same order, as it did before this existed (SONNY-305).
issue_display = {}
issue_order = []

current_name = None
current_text = []
in_vitest_failures = False


def flush():
    global current_name, current_text
    if current_name is not None:
        blocks.setdefault(current_name, []).append("\n".join(current_text))
    current_name = None
    current_text = []


with open(log_path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as handle:
    for raw in handle:
        line = raw.rstrip("\n")

        # vitest first. The heading is checked before the rule that closes an entry, because the
        # heading is itself a rule and would otherwise be read as one.
        if vitest_failures_heading_re.match(line):
            flush()
            in_vitest_failures = True
            continue
        if vitest_summary_re.match(line):
            flush()
            in_vitest_failures = False
            continue
        # Its FAIL line both names a failing test and opens that test's block, so it does the work
        # swift-testing splits across two line shapes. There is no separate per-test summary line to
        # merge in afterwards.
        vitest = vitest_fail_re.match(line) if in_vitest_failures else None
        if vitest:
            flush()
            current_name = vitest.group(1)
            if current_name not in failing:
                failing[current_name] = current_name
                order.append(current_name)
            # Empty, not seeded with the name: a signature matches a failure's MESSAGE, and the text
            # on this line is what failed, not why.
            current_text = []
            continue
        if vitest_boundary_re.match(line):
            flush()
            continue

        match = issue_re.search(line)
        if match:
            flush()
            current_name = base(match.group(1))
            if current_name not in issue_display:
                issue_display[current_name] = match.group(1)
                issue_order.append(current_name)
            current_text = [match.group(2)]
            continue
        if boundary_re.search(line):
            flush()
        elif current_name is not None:
            current_text.append(line)
        failure = failed_re.match(line)
        if failure:
            name = failure.group(1)
            if name.startswith("run with "):
                continue
            name = re.sub(r" with \d+ test cases$", "", name)
            key = base(name)
            if key not in failing:
                failing[key] = name
                order.append(key)
flush()

# The two log shapes are not redundant, and reading only the second is how a kill loses the name of
# the test that made it. A test whose issues were printed and whose failure line was not is failing
# — it recorded an issue, which is what failing means — so it joins the list under the name its
# issue line used. What it is then classified as is decided below exactly as for any other test: if
# every issue it recorded is declared, it is untrusted, and the mutant comes back UNATTRIBUTED
# rather than KILLED. That direction matters as much as the naming — before this, such a log
# reported a kill with no evidence behind it (SONNY-305).
for key in issue_order:
    if key not in failing:
        failing[key] = issue_display[key]
        order.append(key)

for key in order:
    if key in ALWAYS_TRUSTED:
        print("T\t%s" % failing[key])
        continue
    texts = blocks.get(key, [])
    # A failure with no issue text to read is evidence by default. Nothing declared it, and a
    # classifier that guessed the other way would widen the untrusted column on silence.
    undeclared = not texts
    matched = None
    for text in texts:
        hit = None
        for signature in signatures:
            if signature in text:
                hit = signature
                break
        if hit is None:
            undeclared = True
        elif matched is None:
            matched = hit
    if undeclared or matched is None:
        print("T\t%s" % failing[key])
    else:
        print("U\t%s\t%s" % (failing[key], matched))
PY
}

# A battery whose declaration file has gone missing is a battery back to reporting manufactured
# kills, so it refuses rather than running without one. Same reasoning as every other refusal here:
# the failure it prevents is silent, and a silent failure needs a loud guard.
require_untrusted_declarations() {
  [[ -r "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" ]] ||
    die "cannot read $UNTRUSTED_FILE — the declarations of which failures are not evidence about a
       mutant live there, and without them this harness is back to reporting a flake as a kill."
  # `.*[^[:space:]]` rather than a bare prefix match, because the classifier strips a signature and
  # drops it when nothing is left: a file whose only record read '>>> signature' with nothing after
  # it satisfied the prefix, declared zero signatures, and ran the battery anyway. The Swift suite
  # catches that; this guard exists so the harness need not depend on the suite having been run
  # (PR #112 review, recorded residual).
  if ! grep -q '^>>> signature .*[^[:space:]]' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE"; then
    die "$UNTRUSTED_FILE declares no signatures. An empty declaration file and a deleted one are
       the same thing here: every failure would count as a kill again. A '>>> signature' line with
       nothing after it is empty too — the classifier strips it and drops it — so a file of those
       declares nothing while reading like it declares something."
  fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The battery
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

battery() {
  local plan="$1" only="$2" check_only="$3"

  require_command git
  require_command python3
  require_untrusted_declarations

  local root
  root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  cd "$root"

  # Before require_clean_tree, deliberately, and the order is load-bearing rather than tidy. A
  # battery that is mid-mutant has a dirty tree by construction, so a second run reaching the tree
  # guard first is refused with the wrong message: it prints the mutated file and says "commit or
  # stash the paths above". Telling a second session to commit a mutant is worse than not refusing
  # it at all.
  acquire_lock "$plan"

  MUTATE_SCRATCH="$(mutate_scratch_path "$root")"
  export MUTATE_SCRATCH
  require_isolated_test_command

  require_clean_tree

  local sha branch
  sha="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
  branch="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"

  parse_plan "$plan"

  # Selection: indices to run, in plan order. An unknown id is a typo, not an empty selection.
  local selected=() i j found wanted
  if [[ -z "$only" ]]; then
    i=0
    while [[ $i -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
      selected[${#selected[@]}]=$i
      i=$((i + 1))
    done
  else
    local saved_ifs="$IFS"
    IFS=','
    set -f
    local requested=($only)
    set +f
    IFS="$saved_ifs"
    [[ ${#requested[@]} -gt 0 ]] || die "--only was given an empty list"
    for wanted in "${requested[@]}"; do
      [[ -n "$wanted" ]] || continue
      found=""
      j=0
      while [[ $j -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
        if [[ "${M_ID[$j]}" == "$wanted" ]]; then
          selected[${#selected[@]}]=$j
          found="yes"
        fi
        j=$((j + 1))
      done
      [[ -n "$found" ]] || die "--only names a mutant the plan does not define: $wanted"
    done
    [[ ${#selected[@]} -gt 0 ]] || die "--only selected no mutants"
  fi

  printf '==> %s\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
  printf '    repository : %s\n' "$root"
  printf '    HEAD       : %s (%s)\n' "$sha" "$branch"
  printf '    plan       : %s (%d of %d mutants selected)\n' "$plan" "${#selected[@]}" "$M_COUNT"
  printf '    test cmd   : %s\n' "$TEST_CMD"
  printf '    tree       : clean\n'
  printf '    lock       : held by this run (pid %s) at %s\n' "$$" "$LOCK_DIR"
  printf '    build dir  : %s\n' "$TEST_CMD_ISOLATION"
  printf '\n'

  # Pre-flight before anything runs: every selected mutant must match exactly once. Reported for
  # all of them at once, because fixing a plan one abort at a time is how a session gives up on
  # the plan and hand-rolls a battery again.
  printf '==> Pre-flight: matching every mutant against the tree\n'
  local mismatches=0 count idx
  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    if [[ ! -f "${M_FILE[$idx]}" ]]; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  NO SUCH FILE\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
      continue
    fi
    # Tracked, or the run means nothing. `>>> file ../outside.txt` used to pass pre-flight and be
    # mutated and restored happily, with "Summary at <sha>" printed over a file the SHA does not
    # describe — and `git status` can never see it, so an interrupted run would leave that mutant
    # somewhere the guard will never flag. A gitignored path inside the tree has the same two
    # problems. One check closes both. (PR #70 review, F2.)
    if ! git ls-files --error-unmatch -- "${M_FILE[$idx]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  NOT TRACKED BY GIT — REFUSED\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
      continue
    fi
    count="$(occurrences "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.from")"
    if [[ "$count" == "1" ]]; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  1 match\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    else
      printf '    %-6s %s  %s matches — REFUSED\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$count"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
    fi
  done
  if [[ $mismatches -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    die "$mismatches mutant(s) were refused. Nothing was run and nothing was changed.
       A mutant that does not apply runs an unmutated suite and reports a clean pass, so a bad
       match aborts instead — copy the \`from\` block out of the file again, indentation included.
       A file git does not track cannot be attributed to the SHA this run would print, so it is
       refused too; \`>>> file\` paths are repository-root-relative and must be tracked."
  fi
  printf '\n'

  if [[ -n "$check_only" ]]; then
    printf '==> --check: every selected mutant matches. No tests were run, nothing was changed.\n'
    printf '    This is not a battery result. Re-run without --check to measure anything.\n'
    finish 0
  fi

  # Logs go under .build/, which this repository gitignores — writing them anywhere tracked
  # would dirty the tree the script just insisted was clean, and abort its own next mutant. The
  # property is checked rather than assumed, and a repository that does not ignore .build/ gets
  # its logs outside the working tree instead.
  local stamp logdir
  stamp="$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S)"
  if git check-ignore -q ".build/mutate" 2>/dev/null; then
    logdir=".build/mutate/${sha:0:7}-$stamp"
    mkdir -p "$logdir"
  else
    logdir="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate-logs.XXXXXX")"
    printf '==> .build/ is not gitignored here, so logs go outside the tree:\n    %s\n\n' "$logdir"
  fi

  # A red baseline makes every "kill" below meaningless — the tests were already failing. This is
  # the same class of false measurement the dirty-tree guard exists to stop, so it is not optional.
  printf '==> Baseline: the unmutated tree\n'
  local started elapsed baseline_code=0
  started=$SECONDS
  run_suite "$logdir/baseline.log" || baseline_code=$?
  elapsed=$((SECONDS - started))
  if [[ $baseline_code -ne 0 ]]; then
    # A baseline red *only* on failures this harness does not trust is a different thing from a
    # broken suite, and it used to cost the same: the whole battery, plus the cold build under it.
    # Those failures cannot be attributed to a mutant either — that is what declaring them means —
    # so they cannot manufacture a kill below, and there is nothing for the abort to protect. It
    # still aborts the moment one real assertion is red, which is the case the abort was written for.
    local baseline_class baseline_trusted baseline_untrusted
    baseline_class="$(classify_failures "$logdir/baseline.log")"
    baseline_trusted="$(printf '%s\n' "$baseline_class" | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "T" { print $2 }')"
    baseline_untrusted="$(printf '%s\n' "$baseline_class" | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "U" { print $2 "   [" $3 "]" }')"

    if suite_started "$logdir/baseline.log" && [[ -z "$baseline_trusted" && -n "$baseline_untrusted" ]]; then
      printf '    BASELINE RED, and only on failures this harness does not trust  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      printf '%s\n' "$baseline_untrusted" | sed 's/^/      /'
      printf '    Continuing. None of these is evidence about a mutant, so none of them can\n'
      printf '    manufacture a kill below; every one is excluded from the counts either way.\n'
      printf '    Log: %s\n\n' "$logdir/baseline.log"
    else
      printf '    BASELINE FAILED after %ds — see %s\n' "$elapsed" "$logdir/baseline.log"
      printf '\n'
      failing_tests "$logdir/baseline.log" | sed 's/^/      /'
      printf '\n'
      die "the suite is red before any mutant was applied, so no kill this battery reported could
       be attributed to a mutant. Fix the suite, then re-run."
    fi
  else
    printf '    PASSED  %s  (%ds)\n\n' "$(run_tally "$logdir/baseline.log")" "$elapsed"
  fi

  local killed=0 survived=0 unattributed=0
  local survivor_ids=() unattributed_ids=() outcomes=()
  local backup log code names name_count outcome
  local classification trusted untrusted

  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    printf '==> %s  %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
    printf '    %s\n' "${M_FILE[$idx]}"

    backup="$WORKDIR/backup.$idx"
    cp "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$backup"
    ACTIVE_FILE="${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    ACTIVE_BACKUP="$backup"
    if ! apply_mutant "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.from" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.to"; then
      cp "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      ACTIVE_FILE=""
      die "failed to apply ${M_ID[$idx]} — ${M_FILE[$idx]} was restored from its backup"
    fi

    # Fault-injection seam, set only by `selftest`: reference a genuinely unbound variable so
    # `set -u` kills the script HERE — with the mutant in the file and ACTIVE_FILE set. That
    # placement is the whole point of it. It used to sit before the baseline, where a death leaves
    # cleanup with nothing to restore, so the selftest passed just as happily with
    # `assert_intended_exit` running *ahead* of `cleanup` — the one ordering that abandons the
    # mutant in the tree. Injected here, the ordering is observable and the selftest fails when it
    # is wrong. Do not move it earlier. (PR #70 cycle-3 re-check, G1.)
    if [[ -n "${MUTATE_INJECT_INTERNAL_ERROR:-}" ]]; then
      printf '    (selftest: injecting an unbound-variable death with the mutant applied)\n'
      printf '%s\n' "$MUTATE_A_VARIABLE_THAT_IS_DELIBERATELY_NEVER_SET"
    fi

    log="$logdir/${M_ID[$idx]}.log"
    code=0
    started=$SECONDS
    run_suite "$log" || code=$?
    elapsed=$((SECONDS - started))

    # Restore from the byte-exact copy taken above, not from HEAD. The tree was verified clean
    # before the run, so the two are the same thing here — but a revert that cannot reach past the
    # file it mutated is a revert that cannot destroy anything else either.
    cp "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    cmp -s "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" || die "failed to restore ${M_FILE[$idx]} from its backup"
    ACTIVE_FILE=""
    require_clean_tree_after "${M_ID[$idx]}"

    if [[ $code -eq 0 ]]; then
      outcome="SURVIVED"
      survived=$((survived + 1))
      survivor_ids[${#survivor_ids[@]}]="$idx"
      printf '    SURVIVED — the whole suite passed with this mutant in place  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      printf '    A survivor is a finding: nothing in the suite holds this behaviour.\n'
    elif ! suite_started "$log"; then
      outcome="KILLED (build failure)"
      killed=$((killed + 1))
      # Two ways to reach this, and naming the wrong one is how a report stops being believed. The
      # swift half fails to compile and never prints a line of its own. The server half gets as far
      # as vitest's banner and then loads nothing, because the mutant left a file that will not
      # parse — there is no compiler in that path to blame, so it is not blamed (SONNY-323). A
      # vitest banner in a log that reached this branch means exactly that case: `suite_started`
      # only refuses a banner-carrying log when it also says "no tests".
      if vitest_started "$log"; then
        printf '    KILLED before any test ran — every test file failed to load  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      else
        printf '    KILLED by the compiler — the suite never ran  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      fi
      printf '    No test evidence: this says the mutant does not build, not that it is covered.\n'
    else
      names="$(failing_tests "$log")"
      if [[ -z "$names" ]]; then
        outcome="KILLED (no named tests)"
        killed=$((killed + 1))
        printf '    KILLED — the run failed but named no test  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
        printf '    Read %s; a crash or a harness error looks like this — no test is\n' "$log"
        printf '    named on either line shape, a failure summary or a recorded issue.\n'
      else
        # Which of those names are evidence. A test that failed on a hang backstop or a wall-clock
        # ceiling fails the same way whether this mutant is here or not, so counting it as the
        # thing that caught the mutant is how a battery reports coverage nobody earned. Declared in
        # mutate-untrusted-failures, matched on the issue rather than the test name, so the same
        # test still counts when it fails on a real assertion.
        classification="$(classify_failures "$log")"
        trusted="$(printf '%s\n' "$classification" | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "T" { print $2 }')"
        untrusted="$(printf '%s\n' "$classification" | awk -F'\t' '$1 == "U" { print $2 "   [" $3 "]" }')"

        if [[ -z "$trusted" ]]; then
          outcome="UNATTRIBUTED"
          unattributed=$((unattributed + 1))
          unattributed_ids[${#unattributed_ids[@]}]="$idx"
          printf '    UNATTRIBUTED — the suite went red, and on nothing that is evidence  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
          printf '%s\n' "$untrusted" | sed 's/^/      /'
          printf '    Every failure above is declared as one this harness cannot attribute, so this\n'
          printf '    mutant is NOT counted as killed. It may be covered; nothing here shows that.\n'
        else
          name_count="$(printf '%s\n' "$trusted" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
          outcome="KILLED by $name_count test(s)"
          killed=$((killed + 1))
          printf '    KILLED by %s test(s)  (%ds)\n' "$name_count" "$elapsed"
          printf '%s\n' "$trusted" | sed 's/^/      /'
          if [[ -n "$untrusted" ]]; then
            printf '    Also red, and NOT counted above — declared as not evidence about a mutant:\n'
            printf '%s\n' "$untrusted" | sed 's/^/      /'
          fi
        fi
      fi
    fi
    outcomes[$idx]="$outcome"
    printf '\n'
  done

  printf '==> Summary at %s\n' "$sha"
  printf '    %d mutant(s): %d killed, %d survived, %d unattributed\n' \
    "${#selected[@]}" "$killed" "$survived" "$unattributed"
  printf '    logs: %s\n' "$logdir"
  printf '\n'
  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    printf '    %-6s %-24s %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${outcomes[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
  done
  if [[ $survived -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    printf '    SURVIVORS — each one is a coverage finding, not a harness error:\n'
    for idx in "${survivor_ids[@]}"; do
      printf '      %-6s %s  %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
    done
  fi
  if [[ $unattributed -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    printf '    UNATTRIBUTED — the suite went red and none of it was evidence. These are neither\n'
    printf '    killed nor survived: this battery does not know, and says so rather than guessing:\n'
    for idx in "${unattributed_ids[@]}"; do
      printf '      %-6s %s  %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
    done
  fi
  if [[ $survived -gt 0 || $unattributed -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    printf '    Exiting 2 because of them. Every measurement above was taken at %s.\n' "$sha"
    finish 2
  fi
  printf '\n'
  printf '    Every mutant was killed. Measured at %s.\n' "$sha"
  finish 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# selftest — the acceptance criterion for the guard above.
#
# A guard nobody has watched fire is exactly the class of claim this repository distrusts
# everywhere else, and it is how the previous version of this abort silently stopped existing.
# So: stand up a throwaway git repository, dirty it on purpose, run this same script against it,
# and assert both that it refused and that the uncommitted bytes are still on disk afterwards.
# The real repository is never touched — everything happens under a mktemp directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SELFTEST_ROOT=""
SELFTEST_FAILURES=0
ST_OUT=""
ST_EXIT=0

selftest_cleanup() {
  if [[ -n "$SELFTEST_ROOT" && -d "$SELFTEST_ROOT" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$SELFTEST_ROOT"
  fi
}

st_pass() { printf '    PASS  %s\n' "$1"; }

st_fail() {
  printf '    FAIL  %s\n' "$1" >&2
  SELFTEST_FAILURES=$((SELFTEST_FAILURES + 1))
}

st_eq() {
  if [[ "$2" == "$3" ]]; then st_pass "$1"; else st_fail "$1 — expected [$3], got [$2]"; fi
}

st_contains() {
  case "$2" in
    *"$3"*) st_pass "$1" ;;
    *) st_fail "$1 — output did not contain [$3]" ;;
  esac
}

st_absent() {
  case "$2" in
    *"$3"*) st_fail "$1 — output unexpectedly contained [$3]" ;;
    *) st_pass "$1" ;;
  esac
}

# Runs the real script, with a stand-in for the Swift suite so a selftest needs no toolchain.
#
# ST_OUT is what the REPORT said, with the echoed suite command cut out of it — the header prints
# `test cmd   : $TEST_CMD` and a stand-in is a multi-line shell script, so the whole fixture lands
# in the output before a single mutant runs. Every needle a fixture contains then matches itself,
# and an assertion that the report NAMED a test passes whether or not the report named anything.
# Measured rather than reasoned about: of the checks that existed before this cut, **10 of 68**
# `st_contains … "$ST_OUT"` inside an st_run arm carried a needle that also appears in the stand-in
# that arm ran, and that set is every "the test is named" check in the classifier's arms (scanned
# over this file at 6f604d0, the base this branch sits on; the script is in SONNY-305's closing
# comment, and at c2932b3, with this branch's two new arms, the same scan answers 13 of 75). Both
# figures were re-measured after the rebase onto 6f604d0 rather than translated across it, and both
# came back unchanged. **The figure was first written as 11 of 70 and that was wrong** in the way
# this repository has recorded before: it was taken on a working tree that already carried this
# branch's own first arm, so it counted that arm's own check among the pre-existing ones and
# stamped a working-tree reading with a commit that did not contain it. Commit 7b2b9d0's message
# still says 11 of 70 and is left as written — this repository restamps rather than rewriting a
# commit message, and the correction lives here and in the changelog. (That commit was d1648a2
# before the rebase; renaming the citation is right here because a rebase preserves a commit
# MESSAGE byte for byte, and the message is what is being cited — `git log -1 --format=%B` over
# the two is identical. A figure measured on the old commit's TREE would have had to be re-measured
# instead, which is what the two above were.)
#
# It is the same shape as PR #112's F6, where a check passed with the summary block deleted
# outright, and it was found the same way — a new arm passing a check it had no business passing
# yet.
#
# Cut here rather than by tightening 11 needles, so that the safe thing is what a future arm gets
# by default. The range runs from the `test cmd` line to the `tree` line printed immediately after
# it, and keeps that one; nothing else in the header spills, and the HEAD line carrying the SHA is
# above it.
st_run() {
  local cmd="$1"
  shift
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$cmd" "$SELF" "$@" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  ST_OUT="$(printf '%s\n' "$ST_OUT" \
    | sed '/^    test cmd   : /,/^    tree       : clean$/{ /^    tree       : clean$/!d; }')"
}

selftest() {
  require_command git
  require_command python3

  SELFTEST_ROOT="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate-selftest.XXXXXX")"
  trap 'cleanup; selftest_cleanup; assert_intended_exit' EXIT
  trap 'finish 130' INT TERM

  local root="$SELFTEST_ROOT"
  local sentinel="$root/the-suite-ran.marker"
  mkdir -p "$root/repo"
  cd "$root/repo"

  git -c init.defaultBranch=main init -q .
  git config user.email "mutate-selftest@example.invalid"
  git config user.name "mutate selftest"
  git config commit.gpgsign false

  cat >sample.swift <<'SAMPLE'
enum Period {
    static func label(hour: Int) -> String {
        if hour < 12 { return "Morning" }
        return "Afternoon"
    }

    static func fallback() -> String {
        return "Afternoon"
    }
}
SAMPLE
  git add sample.swift
  git commit -q -m "seed"
  cp sample.swift "$root/sample.pristine"
  local seeded_sha
  seeded_sha="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"

  # Plans live outside the repository: a plan file inside it would itself make the tree dirty.
  cat >"$root/kill.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 the morning boundary widened by an hour
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        if hour < 12 { return "Morning" }
>>> to
        if hour < 13 { return "Morning" }
>>> end
PLAN

  cat >"$root/nomatch.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 a mutant whose text is not in the file
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        if hour < 99 { return "Nowhere" }
>>> to
        if hour < 98 { return "Nowhere" }
>>> end
PLAN

  cat >"$root/ambiguous.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 a mutant whose text appears twice
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        return "Afternoon"
>>> to
        return "Evening"
>>> end
PLAN

  # Stand-ins for the suite, shaped like swift-testing's console output so the report's parsing is
  # exercised too. `kills_the_mutant` fails only while the mutant is in the file, which is what a
  # real killing test does.
  #
  # The line prefixes are symbols, not letters, because that is what swift-testing writes and what
  # the classifier's boundary rule is anchored on: a symbol and two spaces in front of every line
  # the console recorder produces, and never in front of an issue's own message. These fixtures used
  # a literal "X  " — a shape swift-testing emits in no terminal — which made them agree with a
  # boundary rule that fired on the words "Test" and "Suite" anywhere in a line, the defect at
  # PR #112's F3. ◇ ✔ ✘ ↳ are the unicode set; this Mac's runs use SF Symbols glyphs from the
  # private-use area instead, and the rule holds for both because neither set is a letter.
  local always_pass='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local kills_the_mutant='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  # Signals the harness only once the mutant is in the file. Signalling unconditionally would
  # land during the baseline, before anything is mutated, and the restore assertion below would
  # then pass without the restore path ever running.
  local signals_the_harness='if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  kill -TERM $PPID
fi
printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local always_fails='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "✘  Test somethingAlreadyBroken() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
exit 1'

  local records_that_it_ran="touch '$sentinel'
printf \"◇  Test run started.\n\"
printf \"✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n\""

  printf '==> %s selftest\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
  printf '    throwaway repository: %s\n\n' "$root/repo"

  # Every check below runs with the sentinel stand-in, and asserts BOTH the wording only
  # require_clean_tree emits ("refusing to run") and that no suite ran. Without those two, this
  # group passes against a guard that has been deleted outright: require_clean_tree_after also
  # exits 1 and also prints the dirty path, just after both suites have already run against the
  # dirty tree. Measured — with require_clean_tree's git call replaced by an empty string, 2 of
  # this group's 4 checks and the whole untracked group still passed. (PR #70 review, F7.)
  printf '==> The guard: a dirty tree is refused, and the uncommitted work survives\n'
  printf 'let uncommittedWorkInProgress = true\n' >>sample.swift
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a dirty tree exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the refusal comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "the refusal says the tree is not clean" "$ST_OUT" "working tree is not clean"
  st_contains "the refusal prints the dirty path" "$ST_OUT" "sample.swift"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "a suite ran against the dirty tree — the refusal came too late to be this guard"
  else
    st_pass "no suite ran"
  fi
  if grep -q "uncommittedWorkInProgress" sample.swift; then
    st_pass "the uncommitted work is still on disk afterwards"
  else
    st_fail "the uncommitted work was destroyed — this is the bug the script exists to stop"
  fi
  git checkout -- sample.swift

  printf '\n==> The guard covers an untracked file too\n'
  printf 'scratch\n' >"$root/repo/scratch.txt"
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an untracked file exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the refusal comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "the refusal names the untracked file" "$ST_OUT" "scratch.txt"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi

  printf '\n==> status.showUntrackedFiles=no does not silently remove half the guard\n'
  # git status --porcelain honours that config, so setting it used to delete the untracked half
  # of the guard with no edit to this script at all. (PR #70 review, F1.)
  git config status.showUntrackedFiles no
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an untracked file still exits 1 with the config set" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and still comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "and still names the file" "$ST_OUT" "scratch.txt"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi
  git config --unset status.showUntrackedFiles
  rm -f "$root/repo/scratch.txt"

  printf '\n==> The guard covers --check as well — no command tolerates a dirty tree\n'
  printf 'let alsoUncommitted = true\n' >>sample.swift
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan" --check
  st_eq "--check on a dirty tree exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "--check refuses for the same reason" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "--check names the dirty tree" "$ST_OUT" "working tree is not clean"
  git checkout -- sample.swift

  printf '\n==> A mutant whose text does not match is refused before any test runs\n'
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/nomatch.plan"
  st_eq "a non-matching mutant exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the report says how many times it matched" "$ST_OUT" "0 matches — REFUSED"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "the suite ran anyway — a mis-patched mutant would report a clean pass"
  else
    st_pass "the suite never ran"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A mutant whose text appears twice is refused as ambiguous\n'
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/ambiguous.plan"
  st_eq "an ambiguous mutant exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the report says how many times it matched" "$ST_OUT" "2 matches — REFUSED"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "the suite ran anyway"
  else
    st_pass "the suite never ran"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A red baseline aborts instead of attributing kills to mutants\n'
  st_run "$always_fails" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a red baseline exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the abort names the baseline" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"
  st_contains "and says why no kill could be attributed" "$ST_OUT" "red before any mutant was applied"

  printf '\n==> --only rejects an id the plan does not define\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan" --only NOPE
  st_eq "an unknown --only id exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the error names the id" "$ST_OUT" "does not define: NOPE"

  printf '\n==> A killed mutant is reported as killed, with the tests that killed it\n'
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an all-killed battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the kill is counted" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "the killing test is named" "$ST_OUT" "labelIsMorningBeforeNoon()"
  st_absent "a kill is not reported as a survivor" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVED"
  st_contains "the report carries the SHA it measured at" "$ST_OUT" "$seeded_sha"

  printf '\n==> A survivor is loud, distinguishable, and exits non-zero\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a survivor exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the survivor is called out" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVED"
  st_contains "and listed again in the summary" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVORS"
  st_absent "a survivor is not reported as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  # A mutant that TRAPS the test process: the run starts, dies part way through, and never prints a
  # "Test ... failed after" line for anything. `failing_tests` therefore returns nothing — which used
  # to take the whole battery down with it, because `grep -v` exits 1 on empty input and `pipefail`
  # turned that into a failed command substitution under `set -e`. The battery ended at "the harness
  # ended at a point that is not one of its exits", three lines above the arm written to report
  # exactly this, and with nothing above it naming the mutant. That direction under-reports rather
  # than manufacturing a kill, which is why PR #112's reviewer recorded it rather than raising it;
  # it is fixed here because a harness that dies mid-sentence is the extreme case of this branch's
  # own thesis (SONNY-224, founder decision).
  local traps_the_process='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "◇  Suite ProductShellTests started.\n"
  printf "◇  Test theListHasOneRow() started.\n"
  exit 4
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A mutant that traps the test process is reported, not a silent death\n'
  st_run "$traps_the_process" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery finishes and exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the outcome is reported" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED — the run failed but named no test"
  st_contains "and it names the log to read" "$ST_OUT" "a crash or a harness error looks like this"
  st_contains "and says both line shapes were read before saying so" "$ST_OUT" "on either line shape"
  st_contains "and the summary carries it" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED (no named tests)"
  st_absent "the harness did not die mid-sentence" "$ST_OUT" "not one of its exits"

  # The same seam on the baseline, which took the same route out. A baseline that traps has to abort
  # — nothing can be attributed to a mutant that was never applied — but it has to abort by SAYING
  # so, which is what the arm below the failing-test list is for.
  local baseline_traps='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "◇  Suite ProductShellTests started.\n"
exit 4'

  printf '\n==> A baseline that traps aborts by saying so\n'
  st_run "$baseline_traps" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a trapped baseline exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the abort fires" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"
  st_contains "and says the suite was red before any mutant" "$ST_OUT" "red before any mutant was applied"
  st_absent "and does not die mid-sentence either" "$ST_OUT" "not one of its exits"

  # Stand-ins for the two shapes a declared failure arrives in. The first is what `Issue.record`
  # produces: "Issue recorded" on the line naming the test, and the message itself on a continuation
  # line below it — which is the whole reason classify_failures reassembles blocks instead of
  # grepping lines, and the half a line-at-a-time check would silently miss. The second is what
  # `#expect` produces, where the text is on the named line.
  local untrusted_only='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local untrusted_expectation_only='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aLatencyCeiling() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:20:9: Expectation failed: (elapsed → 11.7 seconds) < (.seconds(60) → 60.0 seconds)\n"
  printf "✘  Test aLatencyCeiling() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local kill_beside_untrusted='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:5:9: Expectation failed: (label) == \"Morning\"\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not settle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 2 issues.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  # Red on the unmutated tree, and only on a declared failure; a real kill once the mutant lands.
  local untrusted_baseline='if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
exit 1'

  printf '\n==> A kill whose only evidence is a declared failure is NOT counted as a kill\n'
  # PR #109's R9, reproduced: one test failed, the harness had no way to tell a coincidence from a
  # catch, and it reported a kill. It now reports that it does not know.
  st_run "$untrusted_only" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the failing test is still named" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatNeverSettled()"
  st_contains "and the signature that disqualified it is shown" "$ST_OUT" "did not become idle before timeout"
  # Summary-only text, and the summary's own counter. The check here used to look for
  # "UNATTRIBUTED — the suite went red", which the PER-MUTANT line at the top of this outcome also
  # begins with — so it passed without the summary block existing at all: deleting that block
  # outright still gave a green selftest (PR #112 review, F6). The survivor check this was modelled
  # on is not vacuous only because the word SURVIVORS appears nowhere else.
  st_contains "and it is listed again in the summary" "$ST_OUT" \
              "killed nor survived: this battery does not know"
  st_contains "and the summary counts it as unattributed, not as a kill" "$ST_OUT" \
              "0 killed, 0 survived, 1 unattributed"
  st_absent "it is not reported as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"
  st_absent "and not as a survivor either" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVED"

  printf '\n==> The same holds when the declared text is on the line naming the test\n'
  # `#expect` renders its own expression inline, so this shape never reaches a continuation line.
  # Both are exercised because a classifier that handled only one would pass every other check here.
  st_run "$untrusted_expectation_only" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the failing test is named" "$ST_OUT" "aLatencyCeiling()"
  st_absent "it is not reported as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  # A parameterized test names its issues differently — `recorded an issue with 1 argument mode →
  # .safe at File.swift:9:9:` rather than `recorded an issue at ...`. A classifier written for the
  # plain form matches none of them, so every case of every parameterized test lands in the trusted
  # column and can manufacture a kill exactly as before, with nothing saying so. Found by running
  # the classifier over a real 185-issue log rather than by reading it (SONNY-224); pinned here so
  # it stays found.
  local untrusted_parameterized='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunInEveryMode(mode:) recorded an issue with 1 argument mode → .safe at Fixture.swift:9:9: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunInEveryMode(mode:) with 3 test cases failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A parameterized test names its issues differently, and is classified anyway\n'
  st_run "$untrusted_parameterized" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the parameterized test is named" "$ST_OUT" "aRunInEveryMode(mode:)"
  st_absent "it is not reported as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  # SONNY-305. The third seam, and the ordinary one: a test records every issue it is going to
  # record, and the per-test summary line that names it — "Test <name> failed after ..." — never
  # arrives. The two log shapes are not redundant, and this harness used to read only the second.
  # Measured on the SAME mutant at the SAME tree, twice: inside an eight-mutant battery the log
  # carried five `recorded an issue` lines naming `theBackendSessionKeepsNothingOnDisk()` and zero
  # per-test failure lines, and the mutant was reported "KILLED — the run failed but named no
  # test"; run alone with --only, the same five issue lines arrived with the summary line beside
  # them and the same mutant was reported "KILLED by 1 test(s)", named. So the seam is intermittent
  # rather than a property of a mutant, which is what defeats the obvious check: re-running names
  # the test, the reader concludes flake, and the gap survives. (Root cause of the omitted line is
  # NOT established; nothing here claims one. What is fixed is that the harness reads both shapes.)
  #
  # The fixture keeps the Suite-level failure line, because that one really is emitted and it is
  # also what `boundary_re` closes an issue block on — a stand-in without it would exercise a
  # shape no terminal produces, which is the defect PR #112's F3 came from.
  local issues_but_no_per_test_line='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:5:9: Expectation failed: (label) == \"Morning\"\n"
  printf "✘  Suite PeriodTests failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A kill named only by its issue lines is attributed, not reported as unnamed\n'
  st_run "$issues_but_no_per_test_line" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the kill is counted and attributed" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "and the test that caught it is named" "$ST_OUT" "labelIsMorningBeforeNoon()"
  st_absent "it is not reported as unnamed" "$ST_OUT" "named no test"
  # The half-fix guard, and the reason the fix has two halves rather than the one the ticket
  # proposed. Teach only `failing_tests` to read the issue lines — a second regex of its own, which
  # is what SONNY-305 originally suggested — and it gets past the arm above while
  # `classify_failures` still finds nothing: `trusted` comes back empty, and this same log reports
  # `UNATTRIBUTED — the suite went red, and on nothing that is evidence` above a blank evidence
  # line, with the battery exiting 2 instead of 0. A correctly counted kill turned into a finding,
  # which is worse than the misdiagnosis it replaced. Built and run, not reasoned about: that
  # variant against this fixture prints exactly those two lines and that exit.
  #
  # The shipped fallback delegates instead, so the divergence cannot occur while it stays that way.
  # This check is what notices if someone puts the second regex back.
  st_absent "and not as unattributed either" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"

  # The same seam's other direction, and the one that was a false MEASUREMENT rather than a lost
  # diagnosis. Strip the summary line from a log whose only red is a DECLARED backstop and the
  # classifier saw nothing at all, so it could not report UNATTRIBUTED — the arm above it counted a
  # kill instead. Measured against `main`'s copy of this script: `1 killed, 0 survived, 0
  # unattributed`, exit 0, on a mutant nothing had caught. That is the manufactured kill SONNY-224
  # built the whole declaration mechanism to prevent, reached by a route that mechanism never saw,
  # and it is the reason this counts as a fourth route to a false measurement rather than only as a
  # misdiagnosis. Not observed in the wild — SONNY-305's report is the lost-evidence direction —
  # but reachable, and reproduced here rather than argued.
  local declared_only_and_no_per_test_line='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Suite ProductShellTests failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A declared failure with no summary line is unattributed, not a manufactured kill\n'
  st_run "$declared_only_and_no_per_test_line" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the failing test is named from its issue line" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatNeverSettled()"
  st_contains "and the signature that disqualified it is shown" "$ST_OUT" \
              "did not become idle before timeout"
  st_contains "and the summary counts it as unattributed" "$ST_OUT" \
              "0 killed, 0 survived, 1 unattributed"
  st_absent "it is not counted as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  printf '\n==> A real killer beside a declared failure still kills, and the count excludes the other\n'
  st_run "$kill_beside_untrusted" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "one test killed it, not two" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "the real killer is named" "$ST_OUT" "labelIsMorningBeforeNoon()"
  st_contains "the declared failure is shown separately" "$ST_OUT" "NOT counted above"
  st_contains "and named there" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatNeverSettled()"
  st_absent "the mutant is not unattributed" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"

  # ── The server half (SONNY-323) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # vitest's shapes, copied out of real runs of this repository's own server suite rather than
  # written from memory: `npx vitest run test/screen.test.ts` with src/model/limits.ts made merely
  # wrong, and with the same file made unparseable. Before SONNY-323 every one of these logs was
  # reported "KILLED by the compiler — the suite never ran", which is what SONNY-131's nineteen
  # mutants and SONNY-133's twelve came back as.
  #
  # The rule of U+23AF is vitest's own, and it is what closes an entry — the fixtures carry it for
  # the same reason the swift ones carry the Suite-level failure line: a stand-in exercising a shape
  # no reporter emits is how PR #112's F3 got in.
  local vitest_kills_the_mutant='printf " RUN  v4.1.11 /repo/server\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf " ❯ test/period.test.ts (26 tests | 1 failed) 271ms\n"
  printf "     × labels the hour before noon as morning 3ms\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯ Failed Tests 1 ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯\n"
  printf " FAIL  test/period.test.ts > the period label > labels the hour before noon as morning\n"
  printf "AssertionError: expected \"Afternoon\" to be \"Morning\" // Object.is equality\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[1/1]⎯\n"
  printf " Test Files  1 failed (1)\n"
  printf "      Tests  1 failed | 25 passed (26)\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf " Test Files  1 passed (1)\n"
printf "      Tests  26 passed (26)\n"'

  printf '\n==> A vitest failure is a named kill, not a build failure\n'
  st_run "$vitest_kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the baseline tally is read from vitest's own summary line" "$ST_OUT" \
              "PASSED  26 passed (26)"
  st_contains "the kill is counted and attributed" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "and the test is named with its file and describe block" "$ST_OUT" \
              "test/period.test.ts > the period label > labels the hour before noon as morning"
  st_absent "it is not reported as a build failure" "$ST_OUT" "the suite never ran"
  st_absent "nor as a run that named nothing" "$ST_OUT" "named no test"
  st_absent "nor as unattributed" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"

  # A line a test PRINTED, which is not a failing test however much it looks like one. Measured on
  # this repository's server suite with a throwaway test that logs an entry-shaped line and then
  # fails: vitest echoes a failing test's console output above the error summary, under a
  # `stdout | <file> > <test>` header, and the echoed line arrives at column 0 exactly as a real
  # entry does. Run against this branch's first vitest reader, which had no section gate, this
  # fixture reports KILLED by 2 test(s) and names a test that does not exist — evidence out of
  # nothing, which is the direction that manufactures a kill.
  local vitest_echoes_a_fake_entry='printf " RUN  v4.1.11 /repo/server\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "stdout | test/period.test.ts > the period label > labels the hour before noon as morning\n"
  printf " FAIL  test/made-up.test.ts > a suite > a test that does not exist\n"
  printf " ❯ test/period.test.ts (26 tests | 1 failed) 271ms\n"
  printf "     × labels the hour before noon as morning 3ms\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯ Failed Tests 1 ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯\n"
  printf " FAIL  test/period.test.ts > the period label > labels the hour before noon as morning\n"
  printf "AssertionError: expected \"Afternoon\" to be \"Morning\" // Object.is equality\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[1/1]⎯\n"
  printf " Test Files  1 failed (1)\n"
  printf "      Tests  1 failed | 25 passed (26)\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf " Test Files  1 passed (1)\n"
printf "      Tests  26 passed (26)\n"'

  printf '\n==> A line a test printed is not a failing test, however much it looks like one\n'
  st_run "$vitest_echoes_a_fake_entry" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the real failure is the only one counted" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "and it is the one named" "$ST_OUT" "labels the hour before noon as morning"
  st_absent "the printed line is not named as evidence" "$ST_OUT" "made-up"

  # The other direction, and the one that keeps the arm above from being satisfied by reading any
  # red vitest log as a kill. A mutant that leaves a file the transformer cannot parse gets vitest's
  # banner, a "Failed Suites" heading, a FAIL entry naming the FILE, and "Tests  no tests" — no test
  # ran, so there is no test evidence, and a report claiming a named test caught it would be
  # inventing one out of a load error.
  local vitest_loads_nothing='printf " RUN  v4.1.11 /repo/server\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf " ❯ test/period.test.ts (0 test)\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯ Failed Suites 1 ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯\n"
  printf " FAIL  test/period.test.ts [ test/period.test.ts ]\n"
  printf "Error: Transform failed with 1 error:\n"
  printf "[PARSE_ERROR] Unexpected token\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[1/1]⎯\n"
  printf " Test Files  1 failed (1)\n"
  printf "      Tests  no tests\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf " Test Files  1 passed (1)\n"
printf "      Tests  26 passed (26)\n"'

  printf '\n==> A vitest run that loaded no test file is a build failure, not a named kill\n'
  st_run "$vitest_loads_nothing" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the outcome says nothing ran" "$ST_OUT" "every test file failed to load"
  st_contains "and says what that does and does not prove" "$ST_OUT" "not that it is covered"
  st_contains "and the summary carries it" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED (build failure)"
  st_absent "the file that failed to load is not dressed up as a test that caught it" "$ST_OUT" \
            "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_absent "and no compiler is blamed where there is none" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by the compiler"

  # The whole point of reading vitest at all, rather than only the naming: with nothing classified,
  # mutate-untrusted-failures could not reach the server half, so a flaky test there manufactured a
  # kill exactly as PR #109's R9 did. The signature below is a real record from that file. It is one
  # of the swift half's, because no server-half signature is declared today — which is the answer to
  # the question SONNY-323 asked, whether that file needs a notion of which suite a signature
  # belongs to: it matches the text a failure recorded and knows nothing about what produced it, so
  # a server-half record needs no new mechanism here.
  local vitest_untrusted_only='printf " RUN  v4.1.11 /repo/server\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯ Failed Tests 1 ⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯\n"
  printf " FAIL  test/period.test.ts > the period label > waits for the run to settle\n"
  printf "Error: did not become idle before timeout\n"
  printf "⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[1/1]⎯\n"
  printf " Test Files  1 failed (1)\n"
  printf "      Tests  1 failed | 25 passed (26)\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf " Test Files  1 passed (1)\n"
printf "      Tests  26 passed (26)\n"'

  printf '\n==> A vitest failure on a declared signature is unattributed, the same as a swift one\n'
  st_run "$vitest_untrusted_only" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the failing test is still named" "$ST_OUT" "waits for the run to settle"
  st_contains "and the signature that disqualified it is shown" "$ST_OUT" \
              "did not become idle before timeout"
  st_contains "and the summary counts it as unattributed" "$ST_OUT" \
              "0 killed, 0 survived, 1 unattributed"
  st_absent "it is not counted as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  printf '\n==> A baseline red only on declared failures continues instead of costing the whole run\n'
  st_run "$untrusted_baseline" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery still exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the baseline says what it saw" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE RED, and only on failures this harness does not trust"
  st_contains "and names it" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatNeverSettled()"
  st_contains "and the mutants still ran" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_absent "the abort did not fire" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"

  printf '\n==> A baseline red on anything real still aborts, exactly as before\n'
  st_run "$always_fails" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a genuinely red baseline exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the abort fires" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"
  st_absent "and the continue path does not" "$ST_OUT" "only on failures this harness does not trust"

  # The three below are PR #112's findings, each reproduced against this script before it was fixed
  # and each pinned here. They share a shape worth naming: every one of them made the harness quieter
  # than it should have been, and none of them changed a line of its output.

  # F2. Per-issue, not per-test. The first version ORed over a test's issues, so the declared
  # backstop below threw away the real assertion recorded beside it and the mutant came back
  # UNATTRIBUTED — a kill discarded, and the mutant class most likely to produce this pair is
  # exactly the one that hangs a view model and breaks its assertions.
  local declared_and_real_from_one_test='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatRecordsBoth() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatRecordsBoth() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:12:9: Expectation failed: (label) == \"Morning\"\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatRecordsBoth() failed after 0.001 seconds with 2 issues.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 2 issues.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "◇  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> One test recording a declared failure AND a real one still kills\n'
  st_run "$declared_and_real_from_one_test" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the real assertion is still evidence" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "and the test is named" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatRecordsBoth()"
  st_absent "the declared issue did not disqualify the test that recorded it" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"

  # F3. A declared message that does not fit on one line. The boundary rule used to fire on the
  # words "Test x" or "Suite x" ANYWHERE in a line, so the first line of this message ended its own
  # block and the signature on the second line was never read — reported KILLED, exit 0.
  local declared_message_over_two_lines='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  waited on Suite ProductShellTests to quiesce, then gave up:\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "◇  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A declared message on the second line of an issue is still read\n'
  st_run "$declared_message_over_two_lines" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "and the signature that disqualified it is shown" "$ST_OUT" \
              "did not become idle before timeout"
  st_absent "the first line of the message did not end the block" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  # SONNY-315, found in PR #139's cycle-2 battery at 157ef03. A KNOWN issue is not a failure, and
  # its detail lines are not the message of whatever test was mid-issue when it arrived.
  # HangBackstopTests records one on every run by design, so the ingredient is always present and
  # only the interleaving varies — which is why the battery that reported this saw it once, named
  # the killing test when re-run with --only, and came back clean on the next full run.
  #
  # The wrapped line with no glyph in front of it is not decoration: that is how the real suite
  # prints a message's continuation, measured on a 2305-test run, and it is the line the declared
  # signature sits on.
  local known_issue_between_a_kill_and_its_summary='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:5:9: Expectation failed: (label) == \"Morning\"\n"
  printf "↳  // And it is its own label, not the one before it.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aConditionThatNeverBecomesTrueOnAHealthyActorStillFails() recorded a known issue at HangBackstopTests.swift:174:55: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  timed out after 13.4s waiting for: something that never happens.\n"
  printf "   This deadline is a deadlock backstop, not a timing assertion — it fires only after enough looks.\n"
  printf "↳  the backstop must fire when the condition never becomes true\n"
  printf "✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 19.325 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✔  Test aConditionThatNeverBecomesTrueOnAHealthyActorStillFails() passed after 53.423 seconds with 1 known issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 19.4 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> A concurrent known issue does not excuse the test it interrupted\n'
  st_run "$known_issue_between_a_kill_and_its_summary" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the kill is counted" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "and the test that caught it is named" "$ST_OUT" "labelIsMorningBeforeNoon()"
  st_absent "the known issue did not excuse it" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_absent "and the signature that is not its own is nowhere in the report" "$ST_OUT" \
            "deadlock backstop"

  # The other direction, and the reason this fix is not simply "end a block sooner". The same
  # interleaving around a genuinely DECLARED failure must still excuse it, on the signature IT
  # recorded — a boundary that truncated the interrupted test's own message would turn load into a
  # manufactured kill, which is the failure PR #112's F3 was. Before SONNY-315 this log did reach
  # UNATTRIBUTED, and reached it for the wrong reason: the report named the known issue's signature
  # rather than the failing test's own, so the two assertions below are what separate the fix from
  # the defect.
  local declared_failure_then_a_known_issue='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() recorded an issue at Fixture.swift:10:5: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  View model did not become idle before timeout.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aConditionThatNeverBecomesTrueOnAHealthyActorStillFails() recorded a known issue at HangBackstopTests.swift:174:55: Issue recorded\n"
  printf "↳  timed out after 13.4s waiting for: something that never happens.\n"
  printf "   This deadline is a deadlock backstop, not a timing assertion — it fires only after enough looks.\n"
  printf "✘  Test aRunThatNeverSettled() failed after 1.2 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "✔  Test aConditionThatNeverBecomesTrueOnAHealthyActorStillFails() passed after 53.423 seconds with 1 known issue.\n"
  printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 1.3 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  printf '\n==> and a declared failure it interrupts is still excused, on its own signature\n'
  st_run "$declared_failure_then_a_known_issue" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an unattributed mutant exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the outcome is called out" "$ST_OUT" "UNATTRIBUTED"
  st_contains "the failing test is named" "$ST_OUT" "aRunThatNeverSettled()"
  st_contains "and the signature shown is the one it recorded itself" "$ST_OUT" \
              "did not become idle before timeout"
  st_absent "not the one belonging to the known issue beside it" "$ST_OUT" "deadlock backstop"
  st_absent "it is not counted as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  # F1. The guard on the declaration file cannot be excused by the declaration file. Its failure
  # message quoted the signature it had caught going missing, the classifier matched it, and the
  # battery printed a reassuring line and ran the whole run against a file it had just been told
  # was stale. Named in ALWAYS_TRUSTED rather than matched, and UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests
  # asserts that set is exactly its own tests, so a rename cannot empty it silently.
  local the_declaration_guard_failing='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"
printf "✘  Test everyDeclaredSourceStillAppearsAtTheSiteCountItDeclares() recorded an issue at UntrustedFailureDeclarationTests.swift:120:9: Expectation failed\n"
printf "↳  a declared source no longer appears the number of times it says: the run never finished\n"
printf "✘  Test everyDeclaredSourceStillAppearsAtTheSiteCountItDeclares() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
printf "✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
exit 1'

  printf '\n==> A stale declaration file aborts the battery instead of excusing itself\n'
  st_run "$the_declaration_guard_failing" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery refuses to run" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the abort fires" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"
  st_absent "and the continue arm does not" "$ST_OUT" "only on failures this harness does not trust"
  st_absent "no mutant was measured against a stale file" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  printf '\n==> The declarations are required, and an empty file is the same as a missing one\n'
  # Run a copy of this script from a directory with no declarations beside it. Without this the
  # guard is unwatched, and a deleted mutate-untrusted-failures would put every count back to
  # reporting a flake as a kill with nothing saying so.
  cp "$SELF" "$root/mutate-copy"
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$records_that_it_ran" "$root/mutate-copy" "$root/kill.plan" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  st_eq "a missing declaration file exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and says what is missing" "$ST_OUT" "mutate-untrusted-failures"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi

  : >"$root/mutate-untrusted-failures"
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$records_that_it_ran" "$root/mutate-copy" "$root/kill.plan" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  st_eq "an empty declaration file exits 1 too" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and says why that is the same thing" "$ST_OUT" "declares no signatures"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi

  # And a file that only looks like it declares something. The guard matched the prefix
  # '>>> signature ' while the classifier strips the text and drops it when nothing is left, so a
  # file of bare directives passed the shell guard and ran a battery with zero signatures — every
  # failure a kill again, which is the state this whole mechanism exists to leave (PR #112 review,
  # recorded residual). The record below is complete apart from that.
  printf '>>> signature 
>>> source something
>>> sites 1
>>> reason nothing to match
' \
    >"$root/mutate-untrusted-failures"
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$records_that_it_ran" "$root/mutate-copy" "$root/kill.plan" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  st_eq "a signature line with nothing after it exits 1 too" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and says why an empty one is empty" "$ST_OUT" "declares no signatures"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi
  rm -f "$root/mutate-copy" "$root/mutate-untrusted-failures"

  printf '\n==> The real declaration file is well formed\n'
  # Read as text, like the default-suite-command check below: every signature needs the `source`
  # and the `sites` count that keep it honest in the Swift suite, and the `reason` that makes it
  # reviewable. A record missing one of those is a declaration nobody can check.
  #
  # No pipe between grep and the value being read, and `|| true` on each one, for a reason this
  # repository has written down twice: `grep -c` prints 0 and exits 1 when it matches nothing, so
  # under `set -o pipefail` the assignment failed and killed the selftest three lines above the
  # branch that was supposed to report it. `st_fail "it declares nothing"` had never been reachable
  # (PR #112 review, recorded residual). The digits are then taken with a substitution rather than
  # `tr`, which would put the pipe straight back.
  local signature_count source_count sites_count reason_count
  signature_count="$(grep -c '^>>> signature .*[^[:space:]]' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" || true)"
  source_count="$(grep -c '^>>> source .*[^[:space:]]' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" || true)"
  sites_count="$(grep -c '^>>> sites .*[^[:space:]]' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" || true)"
  reason_count="$(grep -c '^>>> reason .*[^[:space:]]' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" || true)"
  signature_count="${signature_count//[^0-9]/}"
  source_count="${source_count//[^0-9]/}"
  sites_count="${sites_count//[^0-9]/}"
  reason_count="${reason_count//[^0-9]/}"
  if [[ "$signature_count" -gt 0 ]]; then
    st_pass "it declares $signature_count signature(s)"
  else
    st_fail "it declares nothing"
  fi
  st_eq "every signature carries a source to check it against" "$source_count" "$signature_count"
  st_eq "every signature carries the site count that makes the source check bite" \
        "$sites_count" "$signature_count"
  st_eq "every signature carries a reason" "$reason_count" "$signature_count"

  # And none of those counts is zero (SONNY-334). `found == sites` in the Swift guard is satisfied
  # by a scan that finds nothing when a record declares nothing, so a record can be zeroed into
  # protecting nothing while the file still reads as protection — which is the shape the count was
  # added to prevent, through the one door it did not watch. Checked here as well as there for the
  # reason the empty-signature guard above is: this harness must not depend on the Swift suite
  # having been run, and a battery over a server-half diff never runs it at all.
  # A positive count rather than a search for zeros, so that a blank, a negative and a non-numeric
  # value are all refused by the same check instead of one shape at a time.
  local positive_sites
  positive_sites="$(grep -c '^>>> sites [1-9][0-9]*[[:space:]]*$' "$UNTRUSTED_FILE" || true)"
  positive_sites="${positive_sites//[^0-9]/}"
  st_eq "every site count is a positive number, so none of them declares nothing" \
        "$positive_sites" "$signature_count"

  printf '\n==> The default suite command — the one every real battery uses — is isolated\n'
  # Nothing else here can reach this line. Every check in this selftest replaces the suite
  # command with a stand-in, so the default is the one thing a real battery depends on that a
  # selftest never executes — and it is also the line an edit would most plausibly undo, by
  # pasting CLAUDE.md's flagged command back over it. Read as text, because that is what can be
  # checked without a Swift toolchain.
  local default_cmd
  default_cmd="$(sed -n 's/^readonly DEFAULT_TEST_CMD=//p' "$SELF")"
  st_contains "the default builds into the run's own scratch directory" \
    "$default_cmd" '--scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH"'
  st_contains "and keeps its clang module cache inside it" \
    "$default_cmd" 'CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH="$MUTATE_SCRATCH/'
  st_absent "and no longer writes anything into the shared .build/" \
    "$default_cmd" '$PWD/.build/'

  printf '\n==> A scripts/warnings run holding this checkout is refused by name\n'
  # The other half of SONNY-184(c). scripts/warnings compiles the whole tree to count its warnings;
  # a battery starting beside one edits source underneath that build, so its count would describe a
  # tree carrying a mutant. Neither report could show it, so whichever starts second refuses —
  # provided the other's lock is already there. Two starting within milliseconds of each other can
  # both check and both proceed, since the check and the mkdir are not one step; the refusal covers
  # the reachable case, which is one being started beside a run already under way.
  local warnings_decoy="$root/decoy-bin/warnings"
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$warnings_decoy")"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nsleep 60\n' >"$warnings_decoy"
  chmod +x "$warnings_decoy"
  "$warnings_decoy" &
  local warnings_pid=$!
  mkdir -p .git/warnings.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nhead deadbeef\n' "$warnings_pid" \
    >.git/warnings.lock/owner
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the battery is refused" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and names the warnings run, not another battery" "$ST_OUT" \
              "a scripts/warnings run holds this checkout"
  st_contains "and names its lock" "$ST_OUT" "warnings.lock"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "the refused battery took its own lock anyway"
  else
    st_pass "and took no lock of its own"
  fi
  kill "$warnings_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  wait "$warnings_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a dead warnings run's leftover lock blocks nothing" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  rm -rf .git/warnings.lock "$root/decoy-bin"

  printf '\n==> A stale lock does not become a new way to be stuck\n'
  # Three ways a lock goes stale, each recoverable with no hand-edit. A killed run leaves a lock
  # whose pid is gone; a Mac that has wrapped its pid space leaves one whose pid now belongs to
  # something else; and a run killed between the mkdir and the write leaves one with no owner
  # record at all.
  ( exit 0 ) &
  local dead_pid=$!
  wait "$dead_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$dead_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a battery behind a dead owner's lock still runs" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says it cleared a stale lock" "$ST_OUT" "cleared a stale lock"
  st_contains "and says which process is gone" "$ST_OUT" "pid $dead_pid) is gone"

  sleep 30 &
  local other_pid=$!
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$other_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a lock whose pid was reused is not treated as live" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and the reclaim says what the pid is now" "$ST_OUT" "belongs to something else now"
  kill "$other_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  wait "$other_pid" 2>/dev/null || true

  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a lock with no owner record is reclaimed" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says the record is missing" "$ST_OUT" "no owner record"

  printf '\n==> unlock clears what a killed run left, and reports an empty checkout honestly\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" unlock
  st_eq "unlock with no lock exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says there is nothing held" "$ST_OUT" "no run lock is held"
  ( exit 0 ) &
  dead_pid=$!
  wait "$dead_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$dead_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$always_pass" unlock
  st_eq "unlock on a stale lock exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says what it cleared" "$ST_OUT" "cleared a stale lock"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "unlock left the stale lock in place"
  else
    st_pass "the stale lock is gone"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A second battery inside a live one is refused BY THE LOCK, not by the tree guard\n'
  # The stand-in suite re-enters this script from inside the first run — the one moment a second
  # invocation would really interleave with a battery. The exit code alone proves nothing here,
  # and that is the whole reason this group is shaped the way it is: a mutant is applied at that
  # moment, so the tree genuinely IS dirty, and with the lock deleted outright require_clean_tree
  # would still exit 1 on the nested run. It would do it while printing the mutated file and the
  # words "Commit or stash the paths above" — advice that, followed, commits a mutant. So the
  # assertions below are that the refusal carries the lock's wording and carries neither of the
  # dirty-tree guard's two sentences.
  cat >"$root/reenter.sh" <<REENTER
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Stands in for the suite. Re-enters the harness only while the mutant is in the file. The nested
# run gets a suite command of its own so that a broken lock cannot recurse: with the mutant already
# applied its plan matches nothing and its pre-flight refuses.
if grep -q 'hour < 13' sample.swift; then
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"' "$SELF" "$root/kill.plan" \
    >"$root/nested.out" 2>&1
  printf '%s' \$? >"$root/nested.exit"
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='printf "◇  Test run started.\n"' "$SELF" unlock \
    >"$root/nested-unlock.out" 2>&1
  printf '%s' \$? >"$root/nested-unlock.exit"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then printf 'yes' >"$root/lock-survived"; else printf 'no' >"$root/lock-survived"; fi
  printf '◇  Test run started.\n'
  printf '✘  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n'
  printf '✘  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n'
  exit 1
fi
printf '◇  Test run started.\n'
printf '✔  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n'
REENTER
  chmod +x "$root/reenter.sh"
  st_run "$root/reenter.sh" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the outer battery is undisturbed and still kills its mutant" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the outer battery reported its kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_eq "the nested battery exits 1" "$(cat "$root/nested.exit" 2>/dev/null)" "1"
  local nested_out
  nested_out="$(cat "$root/nested.out" 2>/dev/null)"
  st_contains "the nested refusal names the conflict" "$nested_out" "another battery already holds"
  st_contains "and names the run holding it" "$nested_out" "pid     :"
  st_contains "and names the recovery" "$nested_out" "unlock"
  st_absent "the nested refusal is not the dirty-tree guard" "$nested_out" "working tree is not clean"
  st_absent "and never tells anyone to commit a mutant" "$nested_out" "Commit or stash"
  st_eq "the refused run did not delete the live lock" "$(cat "$root/lock-survived" 2>/dev/null)" "yes"
  st_eq "unlock refuses to break a live battery" "$(cat "$root/nested-unlock.exit" 2>/dev/null)" "1"
  st_contains "and says which process still holds it" \
    "$(cat "$root/nested-unlock.out" 2>/dev/null)" "still running a battery"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "the lock outlived the battery that took it"
  else
    st_pass "the lock is released when the battery ends"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after the nested attempt" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""

  printf '\n==> A replacement suite command may not quietly build into the shared .build/\n'
  st_run 'swift test --disable-sandbox' "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a SwiftPM command with no scratch path exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and says what is missing" "$ST_OUT" "names no scratch path"
  st_contains "and hands over the variable to use" "$ST_OUT" "MUTATE_SCRATCH"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "the refused run left its lock behind"
  else
    st_pass "the refused run released its lock"
  fi
  st_run ": swift test --scratch-path \"\$MUTATE_SCRATCH\"
$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the same command carrying a scratch path runs" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and the report says the build directory is this run's own" "$ST_OUT" "this run's own"
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_contains "a stand-in that is not SwiftPM is reported as having nothing to isolate" \
    "$ST_OUT" "no build directory to share"

  printf '\n==> An interrupted battery restores the mutant instead of leaving it in the tree\n'
  # The stand-in suite signals the harness while the mutant is applied — the one moment when an
  # abandoned run would leave mutated source on disk for someone to commit.
  st_run "$signals_the_harness" "$root/kill.plan"
  if [[ $ST_EXIT -ne 0 ]]; then
    st_pass "an interrupted battery exits non-zero (got $ST_EXIT)"
  else
    st_fail "an interrupted battery exited 0"
  fi
  st_contains "the battery had reached the mutant" "$ST_OUT" "the morning boundary widened"
  st_contains "it says what it restored" "$ST_OUT" "interrupted mid-mutant"
  if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
    st_fail "the mutant was left in the working tree"
  else
    st_pass "the mutant is gone from the working tree"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after an interrupt" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "an interrupted battery left its lock behind — every later run would need a reclaim"
  else
    st_pass "an interrupted battery released its lock"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A mutant file git does not track is refused, wherever it points\n'
  # `>>> file ../outside.txt` used to pass pre-flight and be mutated, with "Summary at <sha>"
  # printed over a file that SHA does not describe. A gitignored path had the same hole.
  # (PR #70 review, F2.)
  printf 'outside the repository\n' >"$root/outside.txt"
  printf 'ignored inside the repository\n' >"$root/repo/ignored.txt"
  printf 'ignored.txt\n' >>"$root/repo/.gitignore"
  git add .gitignore
  git commit -q -m "ignore ignored.txt"
  for target in "../outside.txt" "ignored.txt"; do
    cat >"$root/untracked.plan" <<PLAN
>>> mutant M1 a mutant aimed at a file git does not track
>>> file $target
>>> from
outside the repository
>>> to
mutated
>>> end
PLAN
    rm -f "$sentinel"
    st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/untracked.plan"
    st_eq "a mutant aimed at $target exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
    st_contains "$target is reported as untracked" "$ST_OUT" "NOT TRACKED BY GIT"
    if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
      st_fail "a suite ran against $target"
    else
      st_pass "no suite ran for $target"
    fi
  done
  if grep -q "outside the repository" "$root/outside.txt"; then
    st_pass "the file outside the repository was never touched"
  else
    st_fail "a file outside the repository was mutated"
  fi

  printf '\n==> An internal error exits non-zero, and restores the mutant on the way out\n'
  # Two bugs are pinned here. On bash 3.2 the presence of an EXIT trap makes a `set -u` death exit
  # 0, so the harness printed a normal-looking report, died, and returned success (PR #70 review,
  # F6). And the EXIT trap must run `cleanup` BEFORE `assert_intended_exit`, or the exit ends the
  # trap with the mutant still in the tree — which nothing held until the seam was moved to fire
  # with a mutant applied (cycle-3 re-check, G1). The last three checks are the ordering-sensitive
  # ones: reverse the trap and they fail.
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$always_pass" MUTATE_INJECT_INTERNAL_ERROR=1 "$SELF" "$root/kill.plan" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  st_eq "a set -u death inside the harness exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "bash reports the unbound variable" "$ST_OUT" "unbound variable"
  st_contains "and the harness disowns the output above it" "$ST_OUT" "not one of its exits"
  st_contains "the death landed with a mutant applied" "$ST_OUT" "death with the mutant applied"
  st_contains "cleanup ran before the exit, and said so" "$ST_OUT" "interrupted mid-mutant"
  if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
    st_fail "the mutant was left in the tree — cleanup did not run before assert_intended_exit"
  else
    st_pass "the mutant is gone from the working tree"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after an internal error" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "a harness death left its lock behind"
  else
    st_pass "a harness death released its lock"
  fi

  printf '\n==> The file is restored byte for byte and the tree is left clean\n'
  if cmp -s sample.swift "$root/sample.pristine"; then
    st_pass "sample.swift is byte-identical to the committed version"
  else
    st_fail "sample.swift was not restored byte for byte"
  fi
  st_eq "git status --porcelain is empty" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""

  printf '\n'
  if [[ $SELFTEST_FAILURES -eq 0 ]]; then
    printf '==> selftest: all checks passed.\n'
    finish 0
  fi
  printf '==> selftest: %d check(s) FAILED.\n' "$SELFTEST_FAILURES" >&2
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

main() {
  local plan="" only="" check_only="" arg

  case "${1:-}" in
    -h|--help|help)
      usage
      finish 0
      ;;
    selftest)
      [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || die "selftest takes no arguments"
      selftest
      ;;
    unlock)
      [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || die "unlock takes no arguments"
      unlock_command
      ;;
    "")
      usage >&2
      finish 1
      ;;
  esac

  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    arg="$1"
    case "$arg" in
      --only)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "--only needs a comma-separated list of mutant ids"
        only="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
      --check)
        check_only="yes"
        shift
        ;;
      -*)
        die "unknown option: $arg"
        ;;
      *)
        [[ -z "$plan" ]] || die "expected one plan file, got two: $plan and $arg"
        plan="$arg"
        shift
        ;;
    esac
  done

  [[ -n "$plan" ]] || die "no plan file given — scripts/mutate --help"
  # Resolved before battery() changes directory to the repository root.
  [[ "$plan" == /* ]] || plan="$PWD/$plan"

  WORKDIR="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate.XXXXXX")"
  # Called plainly, never as `battery ... || rc=$?`: bash suspends errexit inside a command whose
  # status is being tested, so with that call shape a failing command *inside* battery — a `cp`
  # that cannot write, say — stops failing the run. Execution continues past it and the battery
  # reports a full summary built on a step that did not happen. Measured: with `|| rc=$?` restored
  # and a failing cp injected, the run completes and exits 2; called plainly it dies at exit 1 with
  # no summary.
  #
  # What this does NOT govern is a `set -u` death exiting 0 — that is the EXIT trap, and the
  # sentinel at the top of this file is what fixes it. This comment previously claimed otherwise
  # and the claim was wrong in a way the selftest could not see (PR #70 review, F6).
  battery "$plan" "$only" "$check_only"
}

main "$@"
